DSP.mod(7,{"m7-core-concepts":{"id":"m7-core-concepts","slug":"core-ml-concepts","m":7,"title":"Core ML concepts","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-descriptive"],"tags":["machine-learning","fundamentals"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The core concepts of machine learning: learning paradigms, the bias-variance tradeoff, overfitting and underfitting, regularisation, data splits and cross-validation, and the curse of dimensionality.","keyTerms":["bias-variance tradeoff","overfitting","underfitting","regularization","cross-validation","train validation test","curse of dimensionality","generative vs discriminative"],"interviewQs":["Explain the bias-variance tradeoff.","How do you detect and fix overfitting?","What is the difference between validation and test data?","When is plain k-fold cross-validation wrong?"],"mistakes":["Tuning on the test set","Random k-fold on time series or grouped data","Judging a model by training error"],"practice":"Fit polynomials of degree 1, 3 and 9 to noisy data, compare train and test errors, and explain which is overfit.","related":["m7-leakage","m7-l1-sparsity","m7-tuning"],"points":"Concept - What you need to say\nSupervised / unsupervised / semi / self / RL - Labeled vs unlabeled vs mix vs labels derived from data vs reward-driven.\nClassification vs regression - Discrete vs continuous target.\nBias-variance tradeoff - Error = bias^2 + variance + irreducible noise. High bias = underfitting (too simple); high variance = overfitting (too flexible).\nOverfitting - Low training error, high validation error. Remedies: more data, simpler model, regularization, dropout, early stopping, feature selection, cross-validation, ensembling.\nUnderfitting - High error on both. Remedies: richer model, more features, less regularization, train longer.\nRegularization - Penalizes complexity. L1 (lasso) induces sparsity; L2 (ridge) shrinks weights; ElasticNet mixes both.\nTrain/val/test - Train fits parameters, validation tunes hyperparameters, test is touched once for final estimate.\nCross-validation - k-fold (k = 5 or 10), stratified for classification, TimeSeriesSplit for temporal data, GroupKFold when rows from the same entity must not leak across folds.\nData leakage - Information from outside the training context leaks into the model (see 7.2).\nParametric vs non-parametric - Fixed number of parameters (linear/logistic) vs complexity grows with data (KNN, trees).\nGenerative vs discriminative - Model P(x,y) (Naive Bayes) vs P(y - x) (logistic regression).\nCurse of dimensionality - In high dimensions distances concentrate, data becomes sparse; hurts KNN/clustering."},"intuitive":"<p>A model is a student learning for an exam. A student who only memorises last year&#39;s answers (overfitting) does brilliantly on old questions and badly on new ones. A student who barely studied (underfitting) does badly on everything. The best student understands the ideas well enough to handle new questions. We keep a hidden exam (the test set) that nobody practises on, so we get an honest score. Regularisation is like a teacher telling the student &quot;keep your answers simple&quot;, which stops memorising. Cross-validation is taking several mini-exams to get a steadier score.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Nearly every ML interview question is secretly about generalisation.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Judge models on data they have never seen; balance simplicity against flexibility.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Machine learning learns a function from data. Supervised learning has labels: regression predicts a number, classification a category. Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels. The model&#39;s goal is to generalise to new data. Underfitting means too simple, with high bias and high error everywhere. Overfitting means memorising noise, with low training error and high test error. That is the bias-variance trade-off. Regularisation, more data, simpler models and cross-validation control it. We always split into train, validation and test so the final score is honest. The catch: a high training score alone means nothing, and I always compare it with a held-out score.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Diagnose three models from two numbers each","html":"<p>You trained three models to predict churn. Accuracy on training and validation data:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Train</th><th>Validation</th><th>Gap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A: logistic regression, 3 features</td><td>68%</td><td>67%</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B: depth-25 decision tree</td><td>99%</td><td>71%</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>C: gradient boosting, tuned</td><td>86%</td><td>83%</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Model A: both scores low, small gap.</strong> It is too simple to capture the pattern: <strong>high bias (underfitting)</strong>. Remedy: more informative features or a more flexible model.</li>\n<li><strong>Model B: near-perfect training, much worse validation.</strong> It has memorised the training data, noise included: <strong>high variance (overfitting)</strong>. Remedy: limit the depth, require more samples per leaf, average many trees, or get more data.</li>\n<li><strong>Model C: high validation score, small gap.</strong> The best of the three. A gap of 3 points is normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose by validation, never by training.</strong> B&#39;s 99% means nothing for new customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Before reporting C,</strong> score it once on the untouched <strong>test set</strong>, because tuning on validation data makes the validation score slightly optimistic.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Two numbers, training and validation, diagnose most model problems. Low and low means bias; high and much lower means variance.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-landscape","text":"The landscape","level":3},{"id":"h-bias-variance-and-the-tradeoff","text":"Bias, variance and the tradeoff","level":3},{"id":"h-fixes","text":"Fixes","level":3},{"id":"h-regularisation","text":"Regularisation","level":3},{"id":"h-train-validation-test-and-cross-validation","text":"Train, validation, test, and cross-validation","level":3},{"id":"h-curse-of-dimensionality","text":"Curse of dimensionality","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What you need to say</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supervised / unsupervised / semi / self / RL</td><td>Labeled vs unlabeled vs mix vs labels derived from data vs reward-driven.</td></tr><tr><td>Classification vs regression</td><td>Discrete vs continuous target.</td></tr><tr><td>Bias-variance tradeoff</td><td>Error = bias^2 + variance + irreducible noise. High bias = underfitting (too simple); high variance = overfitting (too flexible).</td></tr><tr><td>Overfitting</td><td>Low training error, high validation error. Remedies: more data, simpler model, regularization, dropout, early stopping, feature selection, cross-validation, ensembling.</td></tr><tr><td>Underfitting</td><td>High error on both. Remedies: richer model, more features, less regularization, train longer.</td></tr><tr><td>Regularization</td><td>Penalizes complexity. L1 (lasso) induces sparsity; L2 (ridge) shrinks weights; ElasticNet mixes both.</td></tr><tr><td>Train/val/test</td><td>Train fits parameters, validation tunes hyperparameters, test is touched once for final estimate.</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-validation</td><td>k-fold (k = 5 or 10), <strong>stratified</strong> for classification, <strong>TimeSeriesSplit</strong> for temporal data, <strong>GroupKFold</strong> when rows from the same entity must not leak across folds.</td></tr><tr><td>Data leakage</td><td>Information from outside the training context leaks into the model (see <a href=\"#/s/7.2\">7.2</a>).</td></tr><tr><td>Parametric vs non-parametric</td><td>Fixed number of parameters (linear/logistic) vs complexity grows with data (KNN, trees).</td></tr><tr><td>Generative vs discriminative</td><td>Model P(x,y) (Naive Bayes) vs P(y</td></tr><tr><td>Curse of dimensionality</td><td>In high dimensions distances concentrate, data becomes sparse; hurts KNN/clustering.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-landscape\">The landscape</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What you need to say</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supervised / unsupervised / semi / self / RL</td><td>Labelled data; no labels; a few labels; labels made from the data itself (for example predict the next word); learning from reward</td></tr><tr><td>Classification vs regression</td><td>Predict a category vs a number</td></tr><tr><td>Parametric vs non-parametric</td><td>Fixed number of parameters (linear, logistic) vs complexity that grows with data (KNN, trees)</td></tr><tr><td>Generative vs discriminative</td><td>Model P(x, y) and get P(y</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-bias-variance-and-the-tradeoff\">Bias, variance and the tradeoff</h3>\n<p>Expected error on new data decomposes as <strong>bias² + variance + irreducible noise</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bias</strong>: error from wrong assumptions (a straight line for a curved truth). High bias = <strong>underfitting</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Variance</strong>: sensitivity to the particular training sample. High variance = <strong>overfitting</strong>: the model chases noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Irreducible noise</strong>: randomness no model can remove.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Complexity reduces bias but raises variance; you want the sweet spot. Drag the polynomial degree and watch training and test error diverge:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"biasvar","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>Real numbers from 20 noisy points of a sine curve (test error on 200 fresh points):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Polynomial degree</th><th>Train MSE</th><th>Test MSE</th><th>Diagnosis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 (a line)</td><td>0.33</td><td>0.32</td><td>underfit: both errors high</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.04</td><td>0.14</td><td>good balance</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.02</td><td>0.12</td><td>about right</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>0.02</td><td>0.15</td><td>starting to overfit: train error tiny, test error rising</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>0.01</td><td>582</td><td>wildly overfit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Diagnosing with learning curves:</strong> if training and validation errors are both high and close, you have <strong>high bias</strong> (need a richer model or features). If training error is low but validation error is much higher, you have <strong>high variance</strong> (need more data, regularisation, or a simpler model).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-fixes\">Fixes</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Remedies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Overfitting</strong> (low train error, high validation error)</td><td>more data, simpler model, regularisation, dropout, early stopping, feature selection, cross-validation, ensembling</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Underfitting</strong> (high error on both)</td><td>richer model, more or better features, less regularisation, train longer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-regularisation\">Regularisation</h3>\n<p>Add a penalty on model complexity to the loss: <strong>L2 / ridge</strong> adds λΣw² (shrinks weights smoothly), <strong>L1 / lasso</strong> adds λΣ|w| (drives some weights to exactly zero, performing feature selection), <strong>elastic net</strong> mixes both. Larger λ means a simpler model with more bias and less variance; choose λ by cross-validation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-train-validation-test-and-cross-validation\">Train, validation, test, and cross-validation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Train</strong> fits the parameters. <strong>Validation</strong> tunes hyperparameters and compares models. <strong>Test</strong> is touched <strong>once</strong>, at the end, for an unbiased estimate. Tuning on the test set is a form of leakage that makes the reported score optimistic.</li>\n<li><strong>k-fold cross-validation</strong> (k = 5 or 10): split the training data into k folds, train on k − 1 and validate on the remaining one, rotate, average. Variants:<ul>\n<li><strong>Stratified</strong> for classification, to keep class ratios in each fold.</li>\n<li><strong>TimeSeriesSplit</strong> for temporal data (always train on the past, validate on the future).</li>\n<li><strong>GroupKFold</strong> when several rows belong to the same entity (customer, patient) that must not appear on both sides.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>See how the folds differ:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"cvfolds","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-curse-of-dimensionality\">Curse of dimensionality</h3>\n<p>In high dimensions data becomes sparse and distances concentrate: the nearest and farthest neighbours are almost equally far, which hurts KNN and clustering. The number of samples needed to cover the space grows exponentially with the number of features. Remedies: feature selection, dimensionality reduction (PCA), regularisation, and models that do not rely on distances.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-core-concepts~1","q":"Training accuracy is 99% and validation accuracy is 71%. What would you try, in order?","a":"<p>A large train-validation gap signals <strong>overfitting</strong>. (1) Rule out bugs: leakage or duplicated rows can also make training look too good or validation look inconsistent, and check that the split mimics production (time or group splits). (2) Reduce complexity: limit tree depth, increase regularisation, prune features. (3) Get more or better data, add augmentation where applicable. (4) Use early stopping and ensembling (bagging). (5) Validate with cross-validation so the estimate is stable. Report validation, not training, performance.</p>\n","s":"It is high variance: check for leakage and duplicates first, then regularise or simplify, get more data, and use cross-validation."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-core-concepts~ca0_1","q":"Train accuracy 99%, validation 78%. Diagnosis and two fixes?","a":"<p>Overfitting (high variance). Add regularisation or reduce complexity, and get more data or use early stopping and cross-validation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-core-concepts~ca0_2","q":"Both train and validation accuracy are 60%. What is wrong?","a":"<p>Underfitting (high bias): use a richer model or better features, reduce regularisation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-core-concepts~ca0_3","q":"Why keep a test set separate from the validation set?","a":"<p>Tuning on validation overfits to it; the untouched test set gives an unbiased final estimate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-core-concepts~ta1_1","q":"<p>A model scores 74% on training data and 73% on validation. Doubling the training data changes the scores to 74% and 74%. What is the diagnosis, and will more data help further?</p>\n","a":"<p>Both scores are similar and modest, and adding data closed the small gap but did not raise the level: this is high bias, not variance. More data will not help much. Improve the model&#39;s capacity or its inputs instead: new features, interactions, or a more flexible model such as gradient boosting, then check whether a gap opens.</p>\n"}]},"m7-l1-sparsity":{"id":"m7-l1-sparsity","slug":"why-l1-gives-sparsity","m":7,"title":"Why L1 gives sparsity","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-core-concepts"],"tags":["machine-learning","regularization"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Why L1 regularisation produces sparse solutions while L2 does not, explained with geometry and gradients, with a numerical demonstration.","keyTerms":["L1 regularization","L2 regularization","sparsity","lasso","ridge","constraint geometry","subgradient"],"interviewQs":["Why does L1 give sparse solutions and L2 does not?","When would you choose ridge over lasso?","What does elastic net do?"],"mistakes":["Saying L1 is better without context","Forgetting to scale features before regularising","Explaining only one of the two arguments (geometry or gradient)"],"practice":"Fit lasso and ridge on data with 10 features and 3 true signals; count non-zero coefficients as the penalty grows.","related":["m7-core-concepts","m8-feature-engineering"],"points":"The L1 constraint region is a diamond with corners on the axes; the loss contours are likely to touch at a corner, making some weights exactly zero. Equivalently, the L1 penalty's gradient has constant magnitude (sign(w)) pushing small weights to zero, while L2's gradient shrinks proportionally to w (never exactly zero)."},"intuitive":"<p>Imagine you have a budget for how big your weights can be. With the L2 rule the budget region is a round ball, so the best answer slides along the smooth edge and nothing is ever exactly zero. With the L1 rule the region is a diamond with sharp corners sitting on the axes; the best answer often lands right on a corner, where some weights are exactly zero. Another way to feel it: L2 is a rubber band that pulls harder the further a weight is (so small weights barely feel it), while L1 pulls with a constant force (so small weights are pushed all the way to zero).</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> &quot;Why does L1 give sparse solutions?&quot; is one of the classic &quot;reasoning&quot; level questions.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> L1 corners and constant push give exact zeros; L2 shrinks smoothly.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Regularisation adds a penalty on the size of the weights. L2, ridge, penalises squared weights and shrinks them smoothly towards zero but rarely to exactly zero. L1, lasso, penalises absolute values and drives some weights exactly to zero, giving feature selection. The geometry explains it: the L1 constraint region is a diamond with corners on the axes, so the loss contour tends to touch a corner, where some weights are zero. The L2 region is a circle with no corners. The gradient view agrees: L1&#39;s pull stays constant, so it can push a small weight all the way to zero, while L2&#39;s pull fades as the weight shrinks. The catch: with correlated features, lasso picks one arbitrarily.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"L1 and L2 on a single weight, by numbers","html":"<p>Take one weight whose unpenalised best value is 0.3, and a penalty strength λ = 1.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The loss without a penalty</strong> is (w − 0.3)², which is smallest at w = 0.3.</li>\n<li><strong>Add an L2 penalty:</strong> (w − 0.3)² + λw². Set the slope to zero: 2(w − 0.3) + 2λw = 0, so w = 0.3 / (1 + λ) = <strong>0.15</strong>. The weight shrinks to half; it does not vanish.</li>\n<li><strong>Add an L1 penalty:</strong> (w − 0.3)² + λ|w|. For w &gt; 0 the slope is 2(w − 0.3) + λ = 0, which gives w = 0.3 − 0.5 = −0.2. That is not positive, so the best point is the corner, <strong>w = 0 exactly</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Now a strong weight, unpenalised best 2.0.</strong> L2 gives 2.0 / 2 = <strong>1.0</strong>. L1 gives 2.0 − 0.5 = <strong>1.5</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unpenalised weight</th><th>L2 (λ = 1)</th><th>L1 (λ = 1)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.3</td><td>0.15</td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>2.0</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li><strong>Read the table.</strong> L1 subtracts a fixed amount (λ/2) and stops at zero, so small weights disappear and big ones survive almost intact. L2 divides by a fixed factor, so every weight shrinks and none reaches zero.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> L1 acts as a feature selector: weak features are switched off entirely. L2 is a gentle, proportional shrink. Scale the features first, or the penalty treats them unfairly.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-argument-1-geometry","text":"Argument 1: geometry","level":3},{"id":"h-argument-2-gradients","text":"Argument 2: gradients","level":3},{"id":"h-see-it-in-numbers","text":"See it in numbers","level":3},{"id":"h-choosing-between-them","text":"Choosing between them","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>The L1 constraint region is a diamond with corners on the axes; the loss contours are likely to touch at a corner, making some weights exactly zero. Equivalently, the L1 penalty&#39;s gradient has constant magnitude (sign(w)) pushing small weights to zero, while L2&#39;s gradient shrinks proportionally to w (never exactly zero).</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-argument-1-geometry\">Argument 1: geometry</h3>\n<p>Regularised regression is equivalent to minimising the loss subject to a <strong>constraint</strong> on the weights: <code>‖w‖₁ ≤ t</code> (L1) or <code>‖w‖₂² ≤ t</code> (L2). The loss contours around the unregularised optimum are ellipses. The solution is where the <strong>smallest ellipse first touches</strong> the constraint region.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>L2 region: a circle (sphere). The ellipse touches it at a generic point; both coordinates are usually non-zero.</li>\n<li>L1 region: a diamond (cross-polytope) with <strong>corners on the axes</strong>. An expanding ellipse typically hits a corner first, where one coordinate is exactly zero. In high dimensions there are many corners and edges, so many weights end up zero.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Move the unconstrained optimum around and change the budget; watch where the solutions land:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"l1l2","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-argument-2-gradients\">Argument 2: gradients</h3>\n<p>The penalty gradient for L2 (<code>λw²</code>) is <code>2λw</code>: proportional to the weight, so as a weight approaches zero the push vanishes (it shrinks but never reaches zero). For L1 (<code>λ|w|</code>) the subgradient is <code>λ·sign(w)</code>: a <strong>constant</strong> pull toward zero regardless of size, so if the data&#39;s pull on a weight is weaker than λ the penalty wins and the weight sticks at exactly 0. The lasso solution is <strong>soft-thresholding</strong>: <code>w = sign(w₀) · max(|w₀| − λ, 0)</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-see-it-in-numbers\">See it in numbers</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Lasso, Ridge\nrng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nX = rng.normal(size=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">200</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>))\nw_true = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, -<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>])      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># only three real signals</span>\ny = X @ w_true + rng.normal(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">200</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> a <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> (<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.01</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2.0</span>):\n    lasso = Lasso(alpha=a).fit(X, y)\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a, (lasso.coef_ != <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;non-zero lasso coefficients&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># alpha 0.01 -&gt; 8 non-zero; 0.1 -&gt; 5; 0.5 -&gt; 3 (exactly the true signals); 1.0 -&gt; 3 (shrunk: 2.13, -1.04, 0.33); 2.0 -&gt; 2</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Ridge keeps all 10 coefficients non-zero at every strength; it only shrinks them.</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>At alpha 0.5 lasso recovers exactly the three real signals and zeroes the rest; beyond that, the penalty starts removing real signals too (bias).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-choosing-between-them\">Choosing between them</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Choice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Many features, expect only a few matter; want feature selection</td><td><strong>Lasso</strong> (L1)</td></tr><tr><td>Many small effects; correlated features; want stability</td><td><strong>Ridge</strong> (L2)</td></tr><tr><td>Groups of correlated features; want sparsity <em>and</em> grouping</td><td><strong>Elastic net</strong> (mix)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With highly correlated features lasso tends to pick one arbitrarily and drop the others (unstable across samples); ridge spreads the weight across them. <strong>Scale your features</strong> before regularising, otherwise the penalty treats large-unit features unfairly. In scikit-learn the penalty is <code>alpha</code> for <code>Lasso</code>/<code>Ridge</code> and <code>C = 1/λ</code> for logistic regression and SVMs (a larger <code>C</code> means weaker regularisation).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-l1-sparsity~1","q":"In a Bayesian view, what priors do L1 and L2 correspond to?","a":"<p>MAP estimation with a <strong>Gaussian</strong> prior on the weights gives an L2 penalty; with a <strong>Laplace</strong> prior (double exponential) it gives an L1 penalty. The Laplace density has a sharp peak at zero and heavier tails than the Gaussian, which expresses the belief &quot;most weights are exactly zero, a few are large&quot;.</p>\n","s":"L2 is a Gaussian prior on the weights; L1 is a Laplace prior (sharply peaked at zero)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-l1-sparsity~ca0_1","q":"You have 10,000 features, most irrelevant. Ridge or lasso, and why?","a":"<p>Lasso (or elastic net): L1 sets many coefficients to exactly zero and selects features.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-l1-sparsity~ca0_2","q":"Why does L2 not give exactly zero weights?","a":"<p>Its gradient is proportional to the weight, so the pull vanishes as the weight nears zero.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-l1-sparsity~ca0_3","q":"What does elastic net combine and why?","a":"<p>L1 and L2 penalties: sparsity plus stability with groups of correlated features.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-l1-sparsity~ta1_1","q":"<p>With λ = 1, what do L1 and L2 do to a weight whose unpenalised value is 0.8? And at what unpenalised value does L1 start setting weights to exactly zero?</p>\n","a":"<p>L2: 0.8 / (1 + 1) = 0.4. L1: 0.8 − 0.5 = 0.3. L1 sets a weight to zero whenever its unpenalised value is at most λ/2 = 0.5 in size, so any weight between −0.5 and 0.5 is switched off. Raising λ widens that band and removes more features.</p>\n"}]},"m7-leakage":{"id":"m7-leakage","slug":"data-leakage-classic-examples","m":7,"title":"Data leakage: classic examples","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-core-concepts"],"tags":["machine-learning","validation","pitfalls"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"What data leakage is, six classic examples, how leakage inflates metrics, and how to prevent it with pipelines and correct splits.","keyTerms":["data leakage","target leakage","train-test contamination","pipeline","out-of-fold target encoding","time-based split","group split"],"interviewQs":["What is data leakage? Give three examples.","Your model has an AUC of 0.99. What do you suspect?","Why must scalers and imputers be fitted on training data only?"],"mistakes":["Preprocessing before splitting","Target encoding without out-of-fold computation","Random splits for temporal or grouped data"],"practice":"Build a leaky and a leak-free pipeline on the same dataset and compare the scores.","related":["m7-tuning","m8-preprocessing","m12-key-concepts"],"points":"1. Scaling/imputing/encoding on the entire dataset before splitting (fit transformers only on train; use Pipeline).\n2. Target encoding computed using the target of the same rows (use out-of-fold).\n3. Features that contain future information (e.g., \"total_refunds_after_purchase\" predicting fraud; using next-month data to predict this month).\n4. Random split for time series or repeated entities (same customer in train and test).\n5. Duplicate rows across train/test.\n6. Using a variable that is a proxy for the label (e.g., \"call_duration\" to predict \"call_answered\").\n\nSignal: suspiciously high validation score (AUC 0.99) or one feature dominating importance."},"intuitive":"<p>Leakage is peeking at the answers before the exam. If any information that would not exist at prediction time sneaks into training, the model looks brilliant in testing and fails in the real world. The sneaky peeks include using next month&#39;s data to predict this month, letting the test set&#39;s averages influence your scaling, or using a column that only gets filled in after the outcome happens. A suspiciously perfect score is usually a clue.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers ask about leakage constantly because it is the most common reason a model works offline but not in production.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> For every feature ask &quot;would I have this at prediction time?&quot; and always split before you fit anything.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Data leakage means the model sees information in training that it will not have at prediction time, so scores look great and real performance collapses. Target leakage: a feature derived from the outcome, like &#39;refund issued&#39; when predicting fraud. Train-test contamination: scaling or imputing on the whole dataset before splitting, or duplicates across the split. Temporal leakage: random splits of time-ordered data, so the model learns from the future. Group leakage: the same customer in both train and test. The fix is to split first, fit every preprocessing step inside a pipeline on training data only, split by time or group when needed, and be suspicious of near-perfect scores. The catch: ask of each feature, will this exist at prediction time?&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Spot the leaks in a feature list","html":"<p>Goal: on 1 June, predict which subscribers will cancel during June. Candidate features:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>When is it known?</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Logins in May</td><td>before 1 June</td><td>safe</td></tr><tr><td>Support tickets, last 90 days</td><td>before 1 June</td><td>safe</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Cancellation reason&quot; field</td><td>only after cancelling</td><td><strong>target leak</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Days until renewal date</td><td>before 1 June</td><td>safe</td></tr><tr><td>Refund issued in June</td><td>after the prediction date</td><td><strong>future leak</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Average spend, computed over the <em>whole</em> year</td><td>includes June</td><td><strong>future leak</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ask one question of every feature:</strong> &quot;On 1 June, would I know this value?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Target leak.</strong> The cancellation reason only exists for people who cancelled; the model learns &quot;reason filled in means churn&quot; and scores near 100% in testing, then fails in use.</li>\n<li><strong>Future leaks.</strong> A June refund and a full-year average contain information from the period you are predicting.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix them.</strong> Drop the reason field; compute every aggregate up to 31 May only.</li>\n<li><strong>Then check the process.</strong> Split the data before scaling or imputing, fit those steps on the training part, and split by time (train on earlier months, test on later ones).</li>\n<li><strong>The warning sign.</strong> If validation accuracy is suspiciously high, sort the features by importance and look at the top one first. It is often the leak.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Leakage is a timing error. Draw the timeline, mark the prediction moment, and allow only information from before it.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-six-classic-examples","text":"The six classic examples","level":3},{"id":"h-a-dramatic-demonstration","text":"A dramatic demonstration","level":3},{"id":"h-prevention-checklist","text":"Prevention checklist","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li>Scaling/imputing/encoding on the <strong>entire dataset</strong> before splitting (fit transformers only on train; use <code>Pipeline</code>).</li>\n<li>Target encoding computed using the target of the same rows (use out-of-fold).</li>\n<li>Features that contain future information (e.g., &quot;total_refunds_after_purchase&quot; predicting fraud; using next-month data to predict this month).</li>\n<li>Random split for time series or repeated entities (same customer in train and test).</li>\n<li>Duplicate rows across train/test.</li>\n<li>Using a variable that is a proxy for the label (e.g., &quot;call_duration&quot; to predict &quot;call_answered&quot;).</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Signal:</strong> suspiciously high validation score (AUC 0.99) or one feature dominating importance.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-six-classic-examples\">The six classic examples</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Preprocessing before splitting.</strong> Scaling, imputing or encoding on the whole dataset before the split leaks the test set&#39;s statistics. Fit transformers on train only (use a <code>Pipeline</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Target encoding on the same rows.</strong> Replacing a category with the target mean computed using the same rows lets the feature contain the label. Use out-of-fold encoding with smoothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Features from the future.</strong> <code>total_refunds_after_purchase</code> predicting fraud, or next month&#39;s activity predicting this month&#39;s churn.</li>\n<li><strong>Random split for time or repeated entities.</strong> The same customer (or adjacent days) in both train and test makes the test too easy.</li>\n<li><strong>Duplicate rows across train and test.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proxy for the label.</strong> <code>call_duration</code> predicting <code>call_answered</code>: a duration above zero implies it was answered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Signals:</strong> suspiciously high validation scores (AUC 0.99), one feature dominating importance, performance collapsing in production.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-dramatic-demonstration\">A dramatic demonstration</h3>\n<p>With <strong>pure noise</strong> (300 rows, 500 random features, random labels) no model can do better than chance (AUC 0.5). But if you select the best 10 features using <em>all</em> the data and only then cross-validate, the model &quot;discovers&quot; signal:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.feature_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SelectKBest, f_classif\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> LogisticRegression\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> cross_val_score\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nX, y = rng.normal(size=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>)), rng.integers(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>)\n\nX_leaky = SelectKBest(f_classif, k=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>).fit_transform(X, y)              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># selection saw ALL labels</span>\ncross_val_score(LogisticRegression(), X_leaky, y, cv=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, scoring=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;roc_auc&quot;</span>).mean()     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.75  (fake!)</span>\n\npipe = make_pipeline(SelectKBest(f_classif, k=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>), LogisticRegression())   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># selection re-done inside every fold</span>\ncross_val_score(pipe, X, y, cv=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, scoring=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;roc_auc&quot;</span>).mean()                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.58  close to chance, as it should be</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>A fake 0.75 AUC from nothing: that is the damage a single leaked step can do. The fix is the <code>Pipeline</code>, which refits every step on the training portion of each fold.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-prevention-checklist\">Prevention checklist</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Split first</strong>, before any fitting step; keep a final test set untouched until the end.</li>\n<li>Put preprocessing inside a <strong>scikit-learn <code>Pipeline</code> / <code>ColumnTransformer</code></strong>, so cross-validation refits it per fold.</li>\n<li>Split by <strong>time</strong> for forecasting (and churn-like problems with a prediction date) and by <strong>group</strong> when an entity has many rows.</li>\n<li>Audit each feature: how and when is it created? Would it exist at prediction time with the same definition?</li>\n<li><strong>De-duplicate</strong> and check for near-duplicates across splits.</li>\n<li>Compare offline performance with a <strong>shadow or canary</strong> deployment, which is the ultimate leakage detector.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-leakage~1","q":"You build a churn model with a feature &quot;days since last login&quot; computed at the time the data was extracted (today). The label is whether the customer churned in the last 30 days. What is wrong?","a":"<p>&quot;Days since last login&quot; at extraction time includes the period in which churn happened; customers who churned have not logged in for a long time <em>because</em> they churned. The feature must be computed <strong>as of the prediction date</strong> (before the label window begins), using only information available then. Rebuild the dataset with a clear <strong>snapshot date</strong>: features from before it, label from the window after it.</p>\n","s":"The feature is measured after the label window, so it leaks the outcome: churned customers have large values by construction."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-leakage~ca0_1","q":"You standardise the whole dataset, then split. What is wrong?","a":"<p>The mean and SD include test rows, leaking test information into training. Fit the scaler on train only, ideally in a Pipeline.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-leakage~ca0_2","q":"Predicting churn, a feature is &#39;days since account closed&#39;. Problem?","a":"<p>Target leakage: it is only known after the churn happens.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-leakage~ca0_3","q":"Forecast sales with a random 80/20 split. Why bad?","a":"<p>Training rows come from after some test rows, so the model uses the future. Split chronologically.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-leakage~ta1_1","q":"<p>You forecast daily sales and evaluate with a random 80/20 split of days. The test error is excellent. Why is it misleading, and what should you do instead?</p>\n","a":"<p>A random split puts later days in the training set and earlier days in the test set, so the model learns from the future (for example, the same week&#39;s pattern on neighbouring days) and the error is optimistic. Split by time: train on, say, January to September and test on October to December, or use several forward-chaining folds. Also make sure lag and rolling features use only past values.</p>\n"}]},"m7-linear-regression":{"id":"m7-linear-regression","slug":"linear-regression","m":7,"title":"Linear regression","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-core-concepts"],"tags":["machine-learning","regression","linear-models"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Linear regression: the model, OLS and the normal equation, assumptions and diagnostics, R-squared and error metrics, multicollinearity, and interpretation.","keyTerms":["ordinary least squares","assumptions","residuals","R-squared","adjusted R-squared","multicollinearity","VIF","homoscedasticity"],"interviewQs":["What are the assumptions of linear regression?","How do you detect and handle multicollinearity?","What does R-squared mean and when is it misleading?","How do you interpret a coefficient?"],"mistakes":["Ignoring residual plots","Interpreting coefficients causally","Comparing R-squared across models with different numbers of features"],"practice":"Fit a line by hand on five points, compute R-squared, then check against scikit-learn.","related":["m7-logistic-regression","m7-l1-sparsity","m3-eda"],"points":"- Model: y = Xw + b; minimize squared error (OLS closed form: w = (X^T X)^-1 X^T y).\n- Assumptions (LINE + no multicollinearity): Linearity, Independence of errors, Normality of residuals (for inference), Equal variance (homoscedasticity), no perfect multicollinearity.\n- Diagnostics: residual plots, QQ plot, Durbin-Watson, VIF (> 5-10 concerning), Breusch-Pagan.\n- Metrics: R^2 = 1 - SSres/SStot; adjusted R^2 penalizes extra features; RMSE/MAE.\n- Fixes: log-transform target, polynomial terms, regularization (ridge for multicollinearity), remove correlated features.\n- Interpretation: coefficient = change in y per unit change in x, holding others constant.\n- Pros: interpretable, fast. Cons: linear only, outlier sensitive."},"intuitive":"<p>Linear regression draws the best straight line (or flat plane) through your data so you can predict a number. &quot;Best&quot; means the total of the squared gaps between the line and the dots is as small as possible. Each slope tells you how much the prediction changes when that input goes up by one, keeping the others fixed. The line is easy to explain, which is why it remains the first model to try and a strong baseline.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers expect you to know its assumptions and diagnostics cold.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Minimise squared errors, check the residuals, beware correlated inputs.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Linear regression fits a line, or a plane, to predict a number as a weighted sum of features plus an intercept. It chooses the weights that minimise the sum of squared errors. Each coefficient is the expected change in the target for a one-unit change in that feature, holding the others fixed. The assumptions are a linear relationship, independent errors, constant error variance, and roughly normal errors for inference; multicollinearity makes coefficients unstable. R squared is the share of variance explained. Residual plots reveal violations. The catch: outliers can pull the line hard, and extrapolating beyond the range of the data is unsafe.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Read a fitted line: coefficients, residuals and R²","html":"<p>A model predicts monthly rent (thousands) from flat size (tens of square metres): <strong>rent = 20 + 3 × size</strong>. Four flats:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>size</th><th>actual rent</th><th>predicted</th><th>residual (actual − predicted)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>52</td><td>50</td><td>+2</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>78</td><td>80</td><td>−2</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>108</td><td>110</td><td>−2</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>142</td><td>140</td><td>+2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The slope, 3:</strong> each extra 10 square metres adds about 3 thousand to the rent.</li>\n<li><strong>The intercept, 20:</strong> the model&#39;s rent at size 0. It is not meaningful by itself (no flat has size 0); it positions the line.</li>\n<li><strong>Residuals:</strong> the vertical gaps between the points and the line. For a least-squares fit they add up to 0 (+2 − 2 − 2 + 2 = 0).</li>\n<li><strong>Sum of squared residuals:</strong> 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16.</li>\n<li><strong>R².</strong> The mean rent is 95. The total squared spread around that mean is 43² + 17² + 13² + 47² = 4,516. R² = 1 − 16 / 4,516 ≈ <strong>0.996</strong>: the line explains 99.6% of the variation in rent.</li>\n<li><strong>Look at the residuals&#39; pattern, not just R².</strong> Here they alternate +, −, −, +, which is no pattern, so the line is a good fit. Residuals that curve, or that spread wider as size grows, would signal a missing curve or unequal variance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A coefficient is &quot;change in the target per unit of the feature, holding the others fixed&quot;. Residuals are where you check whether to trust the line.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-model-and-how-it-is-fit","text":"The model and how it is fit","level":3},{"id":"h-assumptions-line-plus-no-perfect-multicollineari","text":"Assumptions: LINE, plus no perfect multicollinearity","level":3},{"id":"h-metrics","text":"Metrics","level":3},{"id":"h-multicollinearity","text":"Multicollinearity","level":3},{"id":"h-interpretation","text":"Interpretation","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Model:</strong> y = Xw + b; minimize squared error (OLS closed form: w = (X^T X)^-1 X^T y).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions (LINE + no multicollinearity):</strong> Linearity, Independence of errors, Normality of residuals (for inference), Equal variance (homoscedasticity), no perfect multicollinearity.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostics:</strong> residual plots, QQ plot, Durbin-Watson, VIF (&gt; 5-10 concerning), Breusch-Pagan.</li>\n<li><strong>Metrics:</strong> R^2 = 1 - SSres/SStot; adjusted R^2 penalizes extra features; RMSE/MAE.</li>\n<li><strong>Fixes:</strong> log-transform target, polynomial terms, regularization (ridge for multicollinearity), remove correlated features.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpretation:</strong> coefficient = change in y per unit change in x, holding others constant.</li>\n<li><strong>Pros:</strong> interpretable, fast. <strong>Cons:</strong> linear only, outlier sensitive.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-model-and-how-it-is-fit\">The model and how it is fit</h3>\n<p><code>y = w₁x₁ + … + w_d x_d + b + ε</code>, with errors ε. <strong>Ordinary least squares (OLS)</strong> chooses <code>w, b</code> to minimise the sum of squared residuals Σ(yᵢ − ŷᵢ)². There is a closed form, the <strong>normal equation</strong> <code>w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</code> (computed with <code>solve</code>/<code>lstsq</code> in practice), or you can use gradient descent for huge data.</p>\n<p>Five points by hand: x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and y = 2, 4, 5, 4, 5. The slope is cov(x, y)/var(x) = 6/10 = <strong>0.6</strong> and the intercept is 4 − 0.6 × 3 = <strong>2.2</strong>; scikit-learn gives the same and <strong>R² = 0.6</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> LinearRegression\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nx = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.</span>]).reshape(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>); y = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.</span>])\nm = LinearRegression().fit(x, y)\nm.coef_[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>], m.intercept_, m.score(x, y)      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (0.6, 2.2, 0.6)</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-assumptions-line-plus-no-perfect-multicollineari\">Assumptions: LINE, plus no perfect multicollinearity</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Assumption</th><th>Meaning</th><th>How to check</th><th>What to do if violated</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>L</strong>inearity</td><td>the relationship is linear in the parameters</td><td>residuals vs fitted plot (no curve)</td><td>transform features, add polynomial terms, use trees</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I</strong>ndependence of errors</td><td>residuals unrelated to each other</td><td>Durbin-Watson, plot residuals over time</td><td>time-series models, cluster-robust SEs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>N</strong>ormality of residuals</td><td>needed for small-sample inference (CIs, p-values), not for fitting</td><td>QQ plot</td><td>transform target (log), larger n</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E</strong>qual variance (homoscedasticity)</td><td>residual spread constant</td><td>residuals vs fitted (no funnel), Breusch-Pagan</td><td>log the target, weighted least squares, robust SEs</td></tr><tr><td>no perfect multicollinearity</td><td>inputs not exact linear combinations</td><td>correlation matrix, <strong>VIF</strong></td><td>drop or combine features, ridge</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-metrics\">Metrics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>R² = 1 − SS_res / SS_tot</strong>: the share of variance explained (0 to 1 on training data; can be <strong>negative on test data</strong> if the model is worse than predicting the mean). It always rises when you add features, so use <strong>adjusted R²</strong> (penalises extra features) for comparisons, and judge predictive models on held-out data.</li>\n<li><strong>RMSE</strong> (penalises large errors, in the target&#39;s units), <strong>MAE</strong> (robust, same units).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-multicollinearity\">Multicollinearity</h3>\n<p>When inputs are highly correlated, the individual coefficients become <strong>unstable</strong> with inflated standard errors, though predictions can still be fine. Detect with pairwise correlations and the <strong>variance inflation factor</strong>: VIF above 5 to 10 is a concern. Two features that are almost copies (correlation 0.999) produce erratic coefficients from sample to sample:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\"># x2 is x1 plus tiny noise; the true model uses only x1 (coefficient 3)</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># OLS on three different subsamples:   [1.57, 1.23]   [1.67, 1.25]   [0.80, 2.13]   &lt;- the split between x1 and x2 jumps around</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Ridge on the same subsamples:        [1.43, 1.36]   [1.49, 1.42]   [1.33, 1.58]   &lt;- much steadier</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>(The <em>sum</em> of the two coefficients is stable near 3; it is the split that is arbitrary.) Fixes: drop or combine redundant features, use <strong>ridge</strong> regularisation, or PCA.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-interpretation\">Interpretation</h3>\n<p>A coefficient is the expected change in y for a one-unit increase in that feature <strong>holding the other features constant</strong>. It describes association in the data, not causation, and depends on the units (standardise to compare importance). For a log-transformed target, the coefficient is approximately a percentage change.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> interpretable, fast, strong baseline. <strong>Cons:</strong> linear only, sensitive to outliers (squared error), extrapolates blindly.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-linear-regression~1","q":"Your regression&#39;s R² is 0.92 on training data and 0.35 on test data. What happened, and what do you try?","a":"<p>A large drop from train to test signals <strong>overfitting</strong> (too many features, high-degree terms, small data) or <strong>distribution differences</strong> between train and test. Try ridge/lasso, fewer or simpler features, more data, and cross-validation; check for leakage in the training features and whether the test set comes from a different period or population. Always report the held-out metric.</p>\n","s":"Overfitting (or leakage in the training features): simplify, regularise, add data, and re-check the split."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-linear-regression~ca0_1","q":"Coefficient on &#39;square feet&#39; is 150 in a price model. Interpretation?","a":"<p>Holding other features fixed, each extra square foot is associated with a $150 higher predicted price.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-linear-regression~ca0_2","q":"Residuals fan out as predictions grow. Which assumption fails and what can you do?","a":"<p>Constant variance (heteroscedasticity). Transform the target (for example log), use weighted least squares, or robust standard errors.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-linear-regression~ca0_3","q":"Two highly correlated features give wild, flipping coefficients. Why?","a":"<p>Multicollinearity: the model cannot separate their effects. Drop one, combine them, or use ridge.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-linear-regression~ta1_1","q":"<p>A model of daily orders has a coefficient of +15 on <code>is_weekend</code> (1 on Saturday and Sunday, 0 otherwise) and +2 on <code>temperature_c</code>, with an intercept of 100. Predict orders for a Saturday at 30 °C and interpret the weekend coefficient.</p>\n","a":"<p>Prediction = 100 + 15 × 1 + 2 × 30 = 175 orders. Interpretation: holding temperature fixed, a weekend day has about 15 more orders than a weekday. It is an association learned from the data, and it is causal only if nothing else that differs between weekends and weekdays has been left out of the model.</p>\n"}]},"m7-logistic-regression":{"id":"m7-logistic-regression","slug":"logistic-regression","m":7,"title":"Logistic regression","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-linear-regression"],"tags":["machine-learning","classification","linear-models"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Logistic regression: the sigmoid and log-odds, log loss and MLE, interpreting coefficients as odds ratios, thresholds, assumptions, regularisation, and why not linear regression for classification.","keyTerms":["sigmoid","log-odds","odds ratio","cross-entropy","decision threshold","regularization C","multinomial","scorecards"],"interviewQs":["Why logistic regression and not linear regression for classification?","How do you interpret a logistic regression coefficient?","What loss does logistic regression minimise and why?","How do you choose the threshold?"],"mistakes":["Interpreting coefficients as probability changes","Using 0.5 as the threshold by default","Forgetting to scale features when regularising"],"practice":"Fit a logistic regression, convert each coefficient to an odds ratio, and explain one in plain language.","related":["m7-linear-regression","m8-classification-metrics","m1-scratch-ml"],"points":"- Model: P(y=1|x) = sigmoid(w.x + b); log-odds are linear in x.\n- Loss: binary cross-entropy (log loss); trained via MLE/gradient descent (no closed form).\n- Coefficient meaning: exp(w) = odds ratio per unit increase.\n- Threshold: default 0.5, tune using business costs/PR curve.\n- Assumptions: linearity of log-odds, independent observations, little multicollinearity.\n- Extensions: multinomial (softmax), regularized (C = 1/lambda in sklearn).\n- Why not linear regression for classification? Unbounded outputs, not probabilities, sensitive to outliers, violates error assumptions.\n- Used heavily in credit risk (scorecards, WoE/IV) and as a strong baseline."},"intuitive":"<p>Logistic regression answers yes/no questions with a probability. It first works out a score from the inputs (just like linear regression), then squeezes that score through an S-shaped curve so the result is always between 0% and 100%. Big positive scores turn into &quot;almost certainly yes&quot;, big negative ones into &quot;almost certainly no&quot;. You then pick a cut-off: above it you say yes. The weights tell you how strongly each input pushes the odds of &quot;yes&quot; up or down.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> It is the workhorse of credit risk, the baseline for classification everywhere, and a favourite interview topic.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> A linear score, squeezed to a probability; coefficients act on the odds.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Logistic regression predicts a probability for a yes-or-no outcome. It computes a weighted sum like linear regression, then passes it through the sigmoid, which squeezes any number into zero to one. Equivalently it models the log-odds as linear. A coefficient is the change in log-odds per unit; exponentiate it to get an odds ratio. It is trained by maximising likelihood, using log loss, and the decision threshold of fifty per cent is a choice that I tune to the business cost. It gives a linear decision boundary. The catch: it is a classifier by name only in that the model outputs probabilities, and calibration and class imbalance affect them.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"From score to probability to decision","html":"<p>A churn model: score z = −3 + 0.8 × (support tickets in the last 90 days). Probability p = 1 / (1 + e^(−z)).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Compute for four customers.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tickets</th><th>z</th><th>Probability of churn</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>−3.0</td><td>0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>−1.4</td><td>0.20</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.55</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.8</td><td>0.86</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>The S-curve.</strong> The score is a straight line, but the probability bends: it moves slowly near 0 and 1 and fastest around z = 0, where p = 0.5.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the coefficient as odds.</strong> Each extra ticket multiplies the <em>odds</em> of churn by e^0.8 ≈ <strong>2.2</strong>. It does not add a fixed amount to the probability; going from 2 to 4 tickets adds 35 points, going from 4 to 6 adds 31.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the default cut-off.</strong> p = 0.5 when z = 0, that is 0.8 × tickets = 3, so tickets = 3.75. With a 0.5 threshold, customers with 4 or more tickets are flagged.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the threshold from costs.</strong> If a retention call is cheap and losing a customer is expensive, flag at p = 0.15, which means 2 or more tickets (2 tickets give 0.198; 1 ticket gives 0.10). The model stays the same; the decision changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Logistic regression produces a linear score, turns it into a probability, and leaves the decision threshold to you.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-model","text":"The model","level":3},{"id":"h-how-it-is-trained-log-loss-and-mle","text":"How it is trained: log loss and MLE","level":3},{"id":"h-interpreting-coefficients-odds-ratios","text":"Interpreting coefficients: odds ratios","level":3},{"id":"h-the-decision-threshold","text":"The decision threshold","level":3},{"id":"h-assumptions-and-extensions","text":"Assumptions and extensions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Model:</strong> P(y=1|x) = sigmoid(w.x + b); log-odds are linear in x.</li>\n<li><strong>Loss:</strong> binary cross-entropy (log loss); trained via MLE/gradient descent (no closed form).</li>\n<li><strong>Coefficient meaning:</strong> exp(w) = odds ratio per unit increase.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold:</strong> default 0.5, tune using business costs/PR curve.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> linearity of log-odds, independent observations, little multicollinearity.</li>\n<li><strong>Extensions:</strong> multinomial (softmax), regularized (C = 1/lambda in sklearn).</li>\n<li><strong>Why not linear regression for classification?</strong> Unbounded outputs, not probabilities, sensitive to outliers, violates error assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Used heavily in credit risk (scorecards, WoE/IV) and as a strong baseline.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-model\">The model</h3>\n<p><code>P(y = 1 | x) = sigmoid(w·x + b)</code>, where <code>sigmoid(z) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ᶻ)</code>. Equivalently the <strong>log-odds (logit)</strong> is linear: <code>log(p / (1 − p)) = w·x + b</code>. Move the sliders and see how a score becomes a probability:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"sigmoid","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Score z (log-odds)</th><th>Probability</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>−2</td><td>0.119</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0.500</td></tr><tr><td>+2</td><td>0.881</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A probability of 0.8 has odds 4:1 and log-odds <code>ln 4 = 1.386</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-how-it-is-trained-log-loss-and-mle\">How it is trained: log loss and MLE</h3>\n<p>There is no closed form. Fit by maximising the likelihood of the labels, which is the same as minimising <strong>binary cross-entropy (log loss)</strong>:</p>\n<p><code>L = −(1/n) Σ [ yᵢ log pᵢ + (1 − yᵢ) log(1 − pᵢ) ]</code>.</p>\n<p>The loss is <strong>convex</strong> in the weights, so gradient descent finds the global minimum; its gradient is the tidy <code>(1/n) Xᵀ(p − y)</code> (implemented in the from-scratch topic). Confidently wrong predictions (p near 0 when y = 1) are punished heavily.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-interpreting-coefficients-odds-ratios\">Interpreting coefficients: odds ratios</h3>\n<p>Each coefficient is the change in <strong>log-odds</strong> per unit of the feature; <code>exp(w)</code> is the <strong>odds ratio</strong>. Example: a coefficient of 0.69 on &quot;number of late deliveries&quot; gives <code>exp(0.69) ≈ 2</code>, meaning <em>each extra late delivery doubles the odds of churn</em> (holding other features fixed). This is <strong>not</strong> &quot;doubles the probability&quot;: the probability change depends on the starting level (a move from 50% to 67% vs from 1% to 2%).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-decision-threshold\">The decision threshold</h3>\n<p>The default 0.5 is arbitrary. Choose the threshold from business costs: with a costly false negative (missing fraud) lower it; with a costly false positive (blocking a good customer) raise it; or read it off a precision-recall curve to meet a precision target. The model&#39;s <em>ranking</em> (AUC) is independent of the threshold.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-assumptions-and-extensions\">Assumptions and extensions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Linearity of the <strong>log-odds</strong> in the features, independent observations, little multicollinearity, enough events per feature (rule of thumb: 10 events per variable).</li>\n<li><strong>Regularisation:</strong> <code>LogisticRegression(C=...)</code> in scikit-learn where <code>C = 1/λ</code>: smaller <code>C</code> means stronger regularisation; <code>penalty=&quot;l1&quot;</code> for sparse models.</li>\n<li><strong>Multinomial / softmax</strong> for more than two classes.</li>\n<li><strong>Why not linear regression for classification?</strong> It produces unbounded outputs that are not probabilities, is sensitive to outliers, minimises squared error that does not suit a binary target, and violates the error assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit risk:</strong> logistic regression with <strong>Weight of Evidence (WoE)</strong> binned features and <strong>Information Value (IV)</strong> for selection, packaged as <strong>scorecards</strong>, is standard because it is interpretable and regulators accept it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> interpretable, well-calibrated probabilities, fast, strong baseline. <strong>Cons:</strong> linear decision boundary (add interactions or use trees for non-linear structure), needs feature scaling for regularisation.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> LogisticRegression\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StandardScaler\nclf = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(C=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.0</span>, max_iter=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>)).fit(X_train, y_train)\nodds_ratios = np.exp(clf[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>].coef_[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>])          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># per one SD increase of each (standardised) feature</span>\nproba = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]\npred = (proba &gt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.3</span>).astype(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>)               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># a threshold chosen from the PR curve, not the default 0.5</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-logistic-regression~1","q":"A logistic regression coefficient for &quot;monthly_spend&quot; (in thousands of rupees) is −0.4. Interpret it.","a":"<p><code>exp(−0.4) ≈ 0.67</code>: holding other features fixed, each additional ₹1,000 in monthly spend multiplies the <strong>odds</strong> of the positive class (say, churn) by 0.67, a 33% decrease in the odds. The change in probability depends on the starting probability. As with all coefficients, this describes association, not cause, and with regularisation or scaling the units matter.</p>\n","s":"Each extra ₹1,000 of monthly spend multiplies the odds of the event by exp(−0.4) ≈ 0.67 (a 33% reduction in odds)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-logistic-regression~ca0_1","q":"Coefficient 0.69 on &#39;has_coupon&#39;. Interpret.","a":"<p>Odds ratio e^0.69 ≈ 2: having a coupon doubles the odds of the event, holding others fixed.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-logistic-regression~ca0_2","q":"Why not use linear regression for a binary target?","a":"<p>It can predict outside 0–1, errors are not normal or constant, and squared error is a poor fit; logistic uses a bounded link and log loss.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-logistic-regression~ca0_3","q":"The model outputs 0.3 for a fraud case. Is it &#39;not fraud&#39;?","a":"<p>Only under a 0.5 threshold. For costly missed fraud, pick a lower threshold chosen from precision-recall and costs.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-logistic-regression~ta1_1","q":"<p>For a customer with z = 0, what is the churn probability? If this customer gets one more ticket, what are the new odds and the new probability?</p>\n","a":"<p>At z = 0, p = 1 / (1 + e⁰) = 0.5, so the odds are 1 (one to one). One more ticket multiplies the odds by e^0.8 ≈ 2.23, giving odds of 2.23, so p = 2.23 / (1 + 2.23) ≈ 0.69. Equivalently, z = 0.8 and p = 1 / (1 + e^(−0.8)) ≈ 0.69.</p>\n"}]},"m7-decision-trees":{"id":"m7-decision-trees","slug":"decision-trees","m":7,"title":"Decision trees","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-core-concepts"],"tags":["machine-learning","trees"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How decision trees choose splits using Gini or entropy, how to compute information gain by hand, hyperparameters and pruning, and the strengths and weaknesses of single trees.","keyTerms":["Gini impurity","entropy","information gain","pruning","max_depth","min_samples_leaf","variance of trees","regression trees"],"interviewQs":["How does a decision tree choose where to split?","Compute the Gini impurity and information gain of a split.","Why do trees overfit and how do you control it?","Why don't trees need feature scaling?"],"mistakes":["Letting trees grow unconstrained","Expecting trees to extrapolate","Thinking impurity-based importance is unbiased"],"practice":"Compute Gini and entropy for a 6-positive, 4-negative node by hand, then split it and compute the gain.","related":["m7-random-forest","m7-gradient-boosting"],"points":"- Recursive splitting to maximize purity. Gini = 1 - sum p_i^2; Entropy = -sum p_i log2 p_i; information gain = parent impurity - weighted child impurity. Regression trees minimize variance (MSE).\n- Hyperparameters: max_depth, min_samples_split, min_samples_leaf, max_features, ccp_alpha (pruning).\n- Pros: interpretable, handles mixed types, no scaling needed, non-linear, captures interactions. Cons: high variance, overfits, unstable, step-wise predictions, cannot extrapolate."},"intuitive":"<p>A decision tree plays &quot;twenty questions&quot; with your data. It asks the yes/no question that best separates the groups (&quot;is income above 40,000?&quot;), then asks the next best question inside each group, and keeps going until the groups are clean or it runs out of patience. The result is a flowchart that is easy to read and explain. The danger: if you let it ask too many questions it memorises every quirk of the training data.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Trees are the building block of random forests and gradient boosting, the best performers on tabular data.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Split to make groups purer; limit depth to avoid memorising.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A decision tree asks a series of yes-or-no questions on features to split data into purer groups. At each node it picks the split that most reduces impurity, measured by Gini or entropy for classification and variance for regression. It stops at a depth limit or minimum samples, and the leaf predicts the majority class or mean. Trees are easy to read, need no scaling, and capture non-linear interactions. But a deep tree memorises the data, so it overfits, and a small change in data can change the tree. Controls are max depth, minimum leaf size and pruning. The catch: single trees are weak predictors, which is why we ensemble them.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Choose between two splits with Gini","html":"<p>10 customers: 5 churned (C) and 5 stayed (S). Gini impurity = 1 − Σ(share²). The starting node: 1 − (0.5² + 0.5²) = <strong>0.50</strong>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Split A, by plan type.</strong> Monthly plan: 6 customers (5 C, 1 S). Annual plan: 4 customers (0 C, 4 S).\nGini(monthly) = 1 − ((5/6)² + (1/6)²) = 1 − (25 + 1)/36 ≈ 0.278. Gini(annual) = 1 − (0² + 1²) = 0, a pure node.\nWeighted: (6/10) × 0.278 + (4/10) × 0 ≈ <strong>0.167</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Split B, by age under 30.</strong> Under 30: 5 customers (3 C, 2 S). 30 and over: 5 (2 C, 3 S).\nGini of each = 1 − (0.6² + 0.4²) = 0.48. Weighted: <strong>0.48</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gain (drop in impurity).</strong> A: 0.50 − 0.167 = <strong>0.333</strong>. B: 0.50 − 0.48 = <strong>0.02</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The tree picks split A.</strong> Plan type separates churners from stayers far better than age.</li>\n<li><strong>Then it repeats</strong> inside the monthly-plan node (the annual node is already pure), until a stopping rule such as maximum depth or minimum samples per leaf.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A tree is a sequence of greedy choices: at each node, take the split that leaves the children purest. Without a stopping rule it keeps splitting until it memorises the data.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-impurity-and-information-gain-by-hand","text":"Impurity and information gain, by hand","level":3},{"id":"h-growth-overfitting-and-pruning","text":"Growth, overfitting and pruning","level":3},{"id":"h-why-no-scaling-is-needed","text":"Why no scaling is needed","level":3},{"id":"h-weaknesses","text":"Weaknesses","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Recursive splitting to maximize purity. <strong>Gini</strong> = 1 - sum p_i^2; <strong>Entropy</strong> = -sum p_i log2 p_i; <strong>information gain</strong> = parent impurity - weighted child impurity. Regression trees minimize variance (MSE).</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperparameters:</strong> <code>max_depth</code>, <code>min_samples_split</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>, <code>max_features</code>, <code>ccp_alpha</code> (pruning).</li>\n<li><strong>Pros:</strong> interpretable, handles mixed types, no scaling needed, non-linear, captures interactions. <strong>Cons:</strong> high variance, overfits, unstable, step-wise predictions, cannot extrapolate.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-impurity-and-information-gain-by-hand\">Impurity and information gain, by hand</h3>\n<p>A node with proportions <code>pᵢ</code> of each class has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gini impurity</strong> = 1 − Σ pᵢ² (0 for a pure node; 0.5 for a 50/50 two-class node).</li>\n<li><strong>Entropy</strong> = −Σ pᵢ log₂ pᵢ (0 for pure; 1 bit for 50/50).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tree picks the split that maximises <strong>information gain</strong> = impurity of the parent − weighted average impurity of the children.</p>\n<p>Worked example: a node has 10 samples, 6 positive and 4 negative.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parent Gini = 1 − (0.6² + 0.4²) = 1 − 0.52 = <strong>0.48</strong>; parent entropy = <strong>0.971</strong> bits.</li>\n<li>A candidate split gives <strong>left</strong> = 5 samples (4 positive, 1 negative) and <strong>right</strong> = 5 samples (2 positive, 3 negative).<ul>\n<li>Left Gini = 1 − (0.8² + 0.2²) = 0.32. Right Gini = 1 − (0.4² + 0.6²) = 0.48.</li>\n<li>Weighted child Gini = 0.5 × 0.32 + 0.5 × 0.48 = <strong>0.40</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gain = 0.48 − 0.40 = 0.08.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Try your own split:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"tree","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>For <strong>regression trees</strong> the impurity is the variance (mean squared error) of the target in each node, and leaves predict the node&#39;s mean. Continuous features are split at thresholds (the algorithm tries cut points between sorted values); categorical features by groups of categories (scikit-learn needs them encoded).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-growth-overfitting-and-pruning\">Growth, overfitting and pruning</h3>\n<p>The tree keeps splitting until leaves are pure: zero training error and high variance. Control complexity with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>max_depth</code> (most important), <code>min_samples_split</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>, <code>max_features</code>;</li>\n<li><strong>cost-complexity pruning</strong> <code>ccp_alpha</code>, which trims branches that do not improve the error enough to justify their size.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Real data check (2,000 rows, 20 features, cross-validated AUC): an unconstrained tree scores <strong>0.85</strong>, while a depth-4 tree scores <strong>0.91</strong>: constraining the tree <em>improves</em> generalisation.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.tree <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> DecisionTreeClassifier, export_text\ntree = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, min_samples_leaf=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).fit(X, y)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(export_text(tree, feature_names=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">list</span>(X.columns)))        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># a readable rule set</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-why-no-scaling-is-needed\">Why no scaling is needed</h3>\n<p>Splits depend only on the <strong>order</strong> of feature values (a threshold), not their magnitude, so monotonic transformations (scaling, log) change nothing. Trees also handle mixed types, capture <strong>non-linear</strong> effects and <strong>interactions</strong> automatically, and are robust to outliers in the features.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-weaknesses\">Weaknesses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High variance</strong>: small changes in the data can produce a very different tree (hence ensembles).</li>\n<li>Axis-aligned, <strong>step-wise</strong> predictions: poor at smooth or diagonal relationships.</li>\n<li><strong>Cannot extrapolate</strong>: predictions outside the training range stay at the last leaf&#39;s value.</li>\n<li>Impurity-based importance favours high-cardinality and continuous features.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-decision-trees~1","q":"Train accuracy is 100% and validation accuracy is 78% for your decision tree. What do you change?","a":"<p>A fully grown tree memorises the training set. Constrain it (<code>max_depth</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>, <code>ccp_alpha</code>), select features, and validate with cross-validation. If you still need more accuracy, use an <strong>ensemble</strong>: a random forest averages many decorrelated deep trees to cut variance, and gradient boosting fits many shallow trees sequentially to cut bias. Keep the single tree when interpretability is the priority.</p>\n","s":"Reduce depth or increase <code>min_samples_leaf</code>, prune, or move to a random forest or boosting."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-decision-trees~ca0_1","q":"A node has 50 positive and 50 negative. Gini impurity?","a":"<p>1 - (0.5² + 0.5²) = 0.5, the maximum for two classes.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-decision-trees~ca0_2","q":"Name two ways to stop a tree overfitting.","a":"<p>Limit max depth or require a minimum samples per leaf; or post-prune using a complexity penalty.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-decision-trees~ca0_3","q":"Do trees need feature scaling? Why or why not?","a":"<p>No: splits compare a feature to a threshold, so monotonic scaling does not change them.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-decision-trees~ta1_1","q":"<p>A node has 8 samples, 4 positive and 4 negative. A split produces a left child with 3 positive and 1 negative, and a right child with 1 positive and 3 negative. Compute the weighted Gini after the split and the gain.</p>\n","a":"<p>Parent Gini = 1 − (0.5² + 0.5²) = 0.5. Each child has shares 0.75 and 0.25: Gini = 1 − (0.5625 + 0.0625) = 0.375. Both children have 4 samples, so the weighted Gini = 0.5 × 0.375 + 0.5 × 0.375 = 0.375. Gain = 0.5 − 0.375 = 0.125. A useful split, though weaker than one producing a pure child.</p>\n"}]},"m7-random-forest":{"id":"m7-random-forest","slug":"random-forest-bagging","m":7,"title":"Random forest (bagging)","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-decision-trees"],"tags":["machine-learning","trees","ensembles"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How random forests reduce variance by averaging decorrelated trees, OOB error, feature importance and its bias, and hyperparameters.","keyTerms":["bagging","bootstrap sample","random feature subsets","out-of-bag error","permutation importance","variance reduction"],"interviewQs":["How does a random forest reduce variance?","What is out-of-bag error?","Why is impurity-based feature importance biased?","Why random feature subsets at each split?"],"mistakes":["Thinking more trees can overfit","Using impurity importance to rank features with different cardinalities","Expecting random forests to extrapolate"],"practice":"Compare a single tree, a random forest and logistic regression on the same data with cross-validated AUC.","related":["m7-decision-trees","m7-gradient-boosting","m7-ensembling"],"points":"- Train many deep trees on bootstrap samples, using random feature subsets at each split; average (regression) or vote (classification).\n- Reduces variance without raising bias much; decorrelated trees give better averaging.\n- OOB error: free validation using rows left out of each bootstrap.\n- Feature importance: impurity-based (biased toward high-cardinality) vs permutation importance (preferred).\n- Hyperparameters: n_estimators, max_depth, max_features, min_samples_leaf.\n- Pros: robust, strong default, little tuning. Cons: large, slower inference, weaker at extrapolation, less interpretable."},"intuitive":"<p>One tree can be moody: change the data a little and it changes its mind. A random forest asks hundreds of trees and takes a vote. Each tree sees a slightly different random sample of the rows and only a random handful of the columns at each decision, so the trees make different mistakes, and mistakes that differ tend to cancel out in the average. It is like asking a large panel of slightly different experts instead of trusting one.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A robust, low-tuning default for tabular data, and the classic example of &quot;reduce variance by averaging&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Many decorrelated, deep trees, averaged: lower variance without much extra bias.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A random forest averages many decision trees. Each tree is trained on a bootstrap sample, a random draw with replacement of the rows, and at each split considers only a random subset of the features. That makes the trees different from each other, and averaging different, noisy-but-unbiased trees reduces variance without raising bias much. For classification the trees vote. Out-of-bag samples, the rows left out of each bootstrap, give a free validation score. Feature importance shows which features matter. It needs little tuning, handles non-linear patterns, and rarely overfits badly. The catch: it is less interpretable than one tree, slower to predict, and bad at extrapolating beyond the training range.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Why averaging trees helps","html":"<p>A house&#39;s true price is 305 (thousands). Three trees, each trained on a different bootstrap sample, predict 280, 320 and 330.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Each tree is noisy.</strong> Their errors are −25, +15 and +25: each tree has latched onto quirks of its own sample.</li>\n<li><strong>Average them:</strong> (280 + 320 + 330) / 3 = <strong>310</strong>, an error of +5. The errors partly cancel because they point in different directions.</li>\n<li><strong>Why they differ.</strong> Each tree sees a bootstrap sample (rows drawn with replacement), and each split considers only a random subset of features. Different data and different options give different mistakes.</li>\n<li><strong>The cancelling only works if the trees disagree.</strong> If all three said 330, the average would be 330. Diversity is the point.</li>\n<li><strong>Out-of-bag rows.</strong> A bootstrap sample of n rows misses each row with probability (1 − 1/n)ⁿ ≈ 0.37, so about 37% of rows were not seen by a given tree. Scoring each row with the trees that did not see it gives a free validation estimate, the OOB score.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Averaging many independently wrong models lowers variance. A random forest makes its trees independent on purpose.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-recipe-bagging-plus-random-features","text":"The recipe (bagging plus random features)","level":3},{"id":"h-out-of-bag-oob-error","text":"Out-of-bag (OOB) error","level":3},{"id":"h-feature-importance-two-kinds","text":"Feature importance: two kinds","level":3},{"id":"h-hyperparameters-and-trade-offs","text":"Hyperparameters and trade-offs","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Train many deep trees on <strong>bootstrap samples</strong>, using <strong>random feature subsets</strong> at each split; average (regression) or vote (classification).</li>\n<li>Reduces <strong>variance</strong> without raising bias much; decorrelated trees give better averaging.</li>\n<li><strong>OOB error:</strong> free validation using rows left out of each bootstrap.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature importance:</strong> impurity-based (biased toward high-cardinality) vs permutation importance (preferred).</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperparameters:</strong> <code>n_estimators</code>, <code>max_depth</code>, <code>max_features</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pros:</strong> robust, strong default, little tuning. <strong>Cons:</strong> large, slower inference, weaker at extrapolation, less interpretable.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-recipe-bagging-plus-random-features\">The recipe (bagging plus random features)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw a <strong>bootstrap sample</strong> (rows sampled with replacement) for each tree.</li>\n<li>Grow a deep tree, but at <strong>each split consider only a random subset of features</strong> (<code>max_features</code>, often √d for classification).</li>\n<li><strong>Average</strong> predictions (regression) or <strong>vote</strong> (classification).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why averaging helps:</strong> the variance of the average of B trees with variance σ² and pairwise correlation ρ is <code>ρσ² + (1 − ρ)σ²/B</code>. More trees shrink the second term; the <strong>random feature subsets reduce ρ</strong> (the correlation between trees), which is what makes the average much better than plain bagging. Bias is about the same as a single deep tree. More trees never cause overfitting; they only cost time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-out-of-bag-oob-error\">Out-of-bag (OOB) error</h3>\n<p>Each bootstrap sample leaves out about <strong>37%</strong> of the rows (the probability a row is never drawn is (1 − 1/n)ⁿ ≈ e⁻¹). Score each row using only the trees that did not see it: a free, nearly unbiased validation estimate without a separate split.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> RandomForestClassifier\nrf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>, oob_score=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).fit(X, y)\nrf.oob_score_                      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># accuracy estimated from out-of-bag rows</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-feature-importance-two-kinds\">Feature importance: two kinds</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Impurity-based</strong> (<code>feature_importances_</code>): how much each feature reduced impurity, summed over splits. Fast but <strong>biased toward continuous and high-cardinality features</strong> (more possible split points give more chances).</li>\n<li><strong>Permutation importance</strong>: shuffle one feature on held-out data and measure how much the score drops. Model-agnostic and less biased; slower; can mislead when features are strongly correlated (shuffling one leaves its twin).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.inspection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> permutation_importance\nr = permutation_importance(rf, X_val, y_val, n_repeats=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, scoring=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;roc_auc&quot;</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-hyperparameters-and-trade-offs\">Hyperparameters and trade-offs</h3>\n<p><code>n_estimators</code> (more is better, with diminishing returns), <code>max_depth</code>, <code>max_features</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>, <code>class_weight=&quot;balanced&quot;</code> for imbalance.</p>\n<p>Same 2,000-row dataset, cross-validated AUC: logistic regression 0.946, single tree 0.852, <strong>random forest 0.959</strong>, gradient boosting 0.956.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> strong default, robust to outliers and unscaled features, handles interactions, little tuning, OOB error. <strong>Cons:</strong> large models, slower inference, less interpretable than one tree, weaker at extrapolation, can be outperformed by boosting when tuned.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-random-forest~1","q":"Why does a random forest choose a random subset of features at each split, and what happens if <code>max_features</code> equals all features?","a":"<p>If one feature is very strong, every bootstrap tree would split on it first and the trees would be highly correlated, so averaging gains little variance reduction. Restricting each split to a random subset forces diversity. With <code>max_features = all</code> you get <strong>bagged trees</strong>, which still help but less. Smaller values reduce correlation but can increase bias because the best feature is sometimes unavailable; tune it.</p>\n","s":"To decorrelate the trees; with all features it becomes plain bagging, where trees are more alike and averaging helps less."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-random-forest~ca0_1","q":"Why does averaging many trees help?","a":"<p>Each tree overfits differently; averaging cancels their independent errors, cutting variance. Diversity from bootstrap and feature subsampling is essential.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-random-forest~ca0_2","q":"What is out-of-bag error?","a":"<p>Each tree is scored on the ~37% of rows not in its bootstrap sample; the combined score is a built-in validation estimate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-random-forest~ca0_3","q":"Random forest trained on prices up to $500 gets a $900 item. What happens?","a":"<p>It predicts near the largest values it has seen; trees cannot extrapolate beyond the training range.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-random-forest~ta1_1","q":"<p>Five trees vote on whether a transaction is fraud: fraud, not fraud, fraud, fraud, not fraud. What does the forest predict, and what &quot;probability&quot; does it report? Why might that probability need calibrating?</p>\n","a":"<p>Three of five vote fraud, so the forest predicts fraud, with a &quot;probability&quot; of 3/5 = 0.6. The vote share measures agreement among the trees, not a calibrated probability: averaged votes tend to cluster away from 0 and 1. If the probability drives a decision, for example an expected-cost threshold, calibrate it on validation data with Platt scaling or isotonic regression.</p>\n"}]},"m7-gradient-boosting":{"id":"m7-gradient-boosting","slug":"gradient-boosting-gbm-xgboost-lightgbm-catboost","m":7,"title":"Gradient boosting: GBM, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-random-forest"],"tags":["machine-learning","trees","ensembles","boosting"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How gradient boosting builds an ensemble of shallow trees that fit the residuals or gradients, key hyperparameters, differences between XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost, and bagging vs boosting.","keyTerms":["boosting","residuals","gradient of the loss","learning rate","shrinkage","XGBoost","LightGBM","CatBoost","early stopping"],"interviewQs":["Explain gradient boosting to a beginner.","Bagging vs boosting?","What are the most important hyperparameters and how do they interact?","How does XGBoost differ from LightGBM and CatBoost?"],"mistakes":["Not using early stopping","Tuning learning rate and number of trees independently","Using boosting without a validation set"],"practice":"Hand-trace two boosting rounds on four data points with a learning rate of 0.5.","related":["m7-random-forest","m7-ensembling","m7-tuning"],"points":"- Boosting: sequentially add shallow trees, each fitting the residuals/gradient of the loss of the current ensemble; reduces bias (and some variance via shrinkage).\n- Key hyperparameters: n_estimators, learning_rate (trade-off with trees), max_depth/num_leaves, subsample, colsample_bytree, min_child_weight, reg_lambda/reg_alpha, early stopping.\n- XGBoost: regularized objective, second-order gradients, handles missing values, column subsampling.\n- LightGBM: histogram-based, leaf-wise growth (faster, may overfit small data), native categorical support.\n- CatBoost: ordered boosting, strong native categorical handling, less tuning.\n- Bagging vs boosting:\n\nBagging (RF) - Boosting (GBM)\nTraining - Parallel, independent - Sequential, dependent\nPrimary effect - Reduces variance - Reduces bias\nBase learners - Deep, high-variance trees - Shallow, weak trees\nOverfitting risk - Low - Higher; needs tuning and early stopping\nOutlier sensitivity - Lower - Higher\n\n- Tabular data reality: GBMs (XGBoost/LightGBM) usually win; start here after a linear baseline."},"intuitive":"<p>Gradient boosting is a team of small learners that fix each other&#39;s mistakes. The first learner makes a rough guess (say, the average). We look at where it was wrong (the errors), and train a second small learner specifically to predict those errors. Adding its corrections to the guess makes the guess better. Then we look at the remaining errors and train a third learner, and so on. Each learner takes only a small step (the learning rate), so the team improves steadily instead of lurching.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost usually win on tabular data; interviewers expect you to explain the idea.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Add shallow trees one at a time, each fit to what the ensemble still gets wrong, with small steps.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Gradient boosting builds trees one after another, each correcting the errors of the sum so far. The first tree makes a rough prediction, the next fits the residuals, meaning the gradient of the loss, and a small learning rate scales its contribution. Many shallow trees add up to a strong model. Random forest reduces variance by averaging independent deep trees; boosting reduces bias by sequentially fixing mistakes. XGBoost adds regularisation and speed, LightGBM grows leaf-wise and uses histograms for speed, CatBoost handles categories natively. The key knobs are learning rate, number of trees, depth, and subsampling. The catch: boosting can overfit noisy data, so I use early stopping on a validation set.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Learning rate versus number of trees, on paper","html":"<p>Suppose each new tree could perfectly fit the current error (residual), and we start with an error of 100. Each round removes a fraction η (the learning rate) of what is left.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>η = 0.5:</strong> after each round the error is multiplied by 0.5: 100 → 50 → 25 → 12.5. Three rounds take it close to 10.</li>\n<li><strong>η = 0.1:</strong> each round keeps 90%: 100 → 90 → 81 → 72.9. It takes <strong>22 rounds</strong> to fall below 10.</li>\n<li><strong>So why use small steps?</strong> Real trees fit the residuals imperfectly, and part of what they fit is noise. Big steps lock in noise quickly; small steps, with many trees, let later trees correct earlier mistakes. Small learning rates usually generalise better.</li>\n<li><strong>The cost</strong> is more trees: more training time and slower predictions.</li>\n<li><strong>The practical recipe:</strong> set a small learning rate (0.05 to 0.1), a large maximum number of trees, and <strong>early stopping</strong>: stop adding trees when the validation score has not improved for, say, 50 rounds.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Learning rate</th><th>Error after 3 rounds</th><th>Rounds to get below 10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.5</td><td>12.5</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>0.1</td><td>72.9</td><td>22</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Learning rate and the number of trees trade off: smaller steps need more trees and usually give a better model. Tune them together, with early stopping.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-algorithm","text":"The algorithm","level":3},{"id":"h-the-hyperparameters-that-matter","text":"The hyperparameters that matter","level":3},{"id":"h-the-libraries","text":"The libraries","level":3},{"id":"h-bagging-vs-boosting","text":"Bagging vs boosting","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Boosting:</strong> sequentially add shallow trees, each fitting the <strong>residuals/gradient of the loss</strong> of the current ensemble; reduces <strong>bias</strong> (and some variance via shrinkage).</li>\n<li><strong>Key hyperparameters:</strong> <code>n_estimators</code>, <code>learning_rate</code> (trade-off with trees), <code>max_depth</code>/<code>num_leaves</code>, <code>subsample</code>, <code>colsample_bytree</code>, <code>min_child_weight</code>, <code>reg_lambda</code>/<code>reg_alpha</code>, early stopping.</li>\n<li><strong>XGBoost:</strong> regularized objective, second-order gradients, handles missing values, column subsampling.</li>\n<li><strong>LightGBM:</strong> histogram-based, leaf-wise growth (faster, may overfit small data), native categorical support.</li>\n<li><strong>CatBoost:</strong> ordered boosting, strong native categorical handling, less tuning.</li>\n<li><strong>Bagging vs boosting:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Bagging (RF)</th><th>Boosting (GBM)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Training</td><td>Parallel, independent</td><td>Sequential, dependent</td></tr><tr><td>Primary effect</td><td>Reduces variance</td><td>Reduces bias</td></tr><tr><td>Base learners</td><td>Deep, high-variance trees</td><td>Shallow, weak trees</td></tr><tr><td>Overfitting risk</td><td>Low</td><td>Higher; needs tuning and early stopping</td></tr><tr><td>Outlier sensitivity</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Tabular data reality:</strong> GBMs (XGBoost/LightGBM) usually win; start here after a linear baseline.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-algorithm\">The algorithm</h3>\n<p>Start with a constant prediction <code>F₀</code> (the mean for regression). For each round m:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Compute the <strong>pseudo-residuals</strong>, the negative gradient of the loss at the current predictions (for squared error this is just <code>y − F(x)</code>).</li>\n<li>Fit a <strong>shallow tree</strong> <code>hₘ(x)</code> to these residuals.</li>\n<li>Update <code>F ← F + η · hₘ</code> where <code>η</code> is the <strong>learning rate</strong> (shrinkage).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A hand-trace: targets <code>y = [10, 20, 30, 40]</code> for inputs 1, 2, 3, 4. <code>F₀ = 25</code>, residuals <code>[−15, −5, 5, 15]</code>. A stump splits between 2 and 3: the left leaf predicts the mean residual −10, the right +10. With <code>η = 0.5</code>: <code>F₁ = 25 + 0.5 × [−10, −10, 10, 10] = [20, 20, 30, 30]</code>; the new residuals are <code>[−10, 0, 0, 10]</code>, and the next tree will fit those. After many rounds the predictions approach <code>y</code>.</p>\n<p>For other losses (log loss for classification, absolute error, quantile), the tree fits the <strong>gradient</strong> of that loss, which is why it is called <em>gradient</em> boosting: it is gradient descent in function space.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-hyperparameters-that-matter\">The hyperparameters that matter</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>n_estimators</code> (rounds) and <code>learning_rate</code></td><td>trade off: smaller rate needs more trees but generalises better; tune together, use <strong>early stopping</strong> on a validation set</td></tr><tr><td><code>max_depth</code> / <code>num_leaves</code></td><td>tree complexity (3 to 8 is typical); deeper captures interactions but overfits</td></tr><tr><td><code>subsample</code>, <code>colsample_bytree</code></td><td>row and column sampling per tree (stochastic boosting): regularises and speeds up</td></tr><tr><td><code>min_child_weight</code> / <code>min_data_in_leaf</code></td><td>minimum evidence required in a leaf</td></tr><tr><td><code>reg_lambda</code>, <code>reg_alpha</code></td><td>L2 / L1 penalties on leaf values</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> lightgbm <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> lgb\nmodel = lgb.LGBMClassifier(n_estimators=<span class=\"hljs-number\">2000</span>, learning_rate=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.03</span>, num_leaves=<span class=\"hljs-number\">31</span>, subsample=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.8</span>, colsample_bytree=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.8</span>)\nmodel.fit(X_tr, y_tr, eval_set=[(X_val, y_val)], eval_metric=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;auc&quot;</span>,\n          callbacks=[lgb.early_stopping(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>)])             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># stops when validation AUC has not improved for 100 rounds</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-libraries\">The libraries</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>XGBoost:</strong> regularised objective, second-order (Newton) gradient information, sparsity-aware handling of missing values (learns a default direction), column subsampling, parallel and distributed.</li>\n<li><strong>LightGBM:</strong> histogram-based splits (fast, low memory), <strong>leaf-wise</strong> growth (lower loss faster but can overfit small data), native categorical handling, very fast on large data.</li>\n<li><strong>CatBoost:</strong> <strong>ordered boosting</strong> (reduces target leakage and prediction shift), excellent native handling of high-cardinality categoricals, often strong with little tuning.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-bagging-vs-boosting\">Bagging vs boosting</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Bagging (random forest)</th><th>Boosting (GBM)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Training</td><td>parallel, independent trees</td><td>sequential, each depends on the last</td></tr><tr><td>Primary effect</td><td>reduces <strong>variance</strong></td><td>reduces <strong>bias</strong> (and some variance via shrinkage)</td></tr><tr><td>Base learners</td><td>deep, high-variance trees</td><td>shallow, weak trees</td></tr><tr><td>Overfitting risk</td><td>low</td><td>higher: needs early stopping and tuning</td></tr><tr><td>Outlier sensitivity</td><td>lower</td><td>higher</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>In practice:</strong> for tabular data start with a regularised linear baseline, then try LightGBM/XGBoost/CatBoost with early stopping; they usually win, but not always on small or very noisy data.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-gradient-boosting~1","q":"You halve the learning rate of a boosting model. What else should you change and why?","a":"<p><code>learning_rate</code> and <code>n_estimators</code> trade off: a smaller step needs more rounds to reach the same fit, but usually generalises better. Increase <code>n_estimators</code> (or set it large and use <strong>early stopping</strong> on a validation set), accept longer training, and re-check other regularisation (subsampling, <code>num_leaves</code>). The standard advice is to choose a small learning rate (0.01 to 0.1), set many trees, and let early stopping decide.</p>\n","s":"Roughly double the number of trees (use early stopping), since each tree now contributes less."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-gradient-boosting~ca0_1","q":"Bagging versus boosting in one line each?","a":"<p>Bagging: train independent models in parallel and average to cut variance. Boosting: train sequentially, each fixing the previous errors, to cut bias.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-gradient-boosting~ca0_2","q":"Lower the learning rate: what else changes?","a":"<p>You need more trees; smaller steps usually generalise better but cost more compute.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-gradient-boosting~ca0_3","q":"Your boosted model&#39;s validation loss starts rising while training loss falls. Action?","a":"<p>Stop at the best iteration (early stopping), or reduce depth/learning rate and add regularisation or subsampling.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-gradient-boosting~ta1_1","q":"<p>With the same idealised setup and η = 0.3, what error remains after 3 rounds, starting from 100? If validation loss is lowest at round 140 and rises after that, how many trees should the final model use?</p>\n","a":"<p>Each round keeps 70%: 100 × 0.7³ = 34.3. For the final model, keep the 140 trees where validation loss was lowest; this is exactly what early stopping does. Training to 500 trees would keep lowering training loss while the model overfits.</p>\n"}]},"m7-svm":{"id":"m7-svm","slug":"support-vector-machines","m":7,"title":"Support vector machines","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-core-concepts"],"tags":["machine-learning","classification","svm"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Support vector machines: margin maximisation, support vectors, the C and gamma hyperparameters, the kernel trick and when SVMs are a good choice.","keyTerms":["margin","support vectors","soft margin","C parameter","kernel trick","RBF kernel","gamma","feature scaling"],"interviewQs":["What is a support vector machine and what are support vectors?","What do C and gamma control?","Why do SVMs need feature scaling?"],"mistakes":["Not scaling features","Using kernel SVMs on very large datasets","Expecting calibrated probabilities"],"practice":"Fit an RBF SVM with a small and a large gamma on two moons data and describe the decision boundaries.","related":["m7-knn","m7-logistic-regression"],"points":"- Find the hyperplane maximizing margin; support vectors define it. Soft margin uses C (high C = less tolerance for errors, risk overfitting).\n- Kernels: linear, polynomial, RBF (gamma controls reach: high gamma = overfitting).\n- Needs feature scaling. Scales poorly beyond ~100k rows. No native probabilities."},"intuitive":"<p>Picture two groups of dots on paper. Many lines can separate them, but the best line is the one with the widest empty street between the groups. That widest-street line is what an SVM finds, and the dots touching the edges of the street are the &quot;support vectors&quot; that hold it in place. If the groups overlap a bit, you allow a few dots to cross the street (soft margin) and pay a penalty. If a straight line is not enough, the &quot;kernel trick&quot; lets you bend the paper so a straight cut becomes a curved boundary.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A classic algorithm that shows up in interviews to test margin, regularisation and kernels.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Widest margin, few support vectors, C trades margin against errors, kernels make curves.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A support vector machine finds the line, or hyperplane, that separates two classes with the widest possible margin, the gap to the nearest points. Only those nearest points, the support vectors, define the boundary. If classes overlap, a soft margin allows some mistakes, controlled by C: large C punishes errors and risks overfitting, small C allows a wider margin. For curved boundaries, the kernel trick implicitly maps data into a higher-dimensional space; the RBF kernel is the common choice with a gamma parameter for how local the boundary is. The catch: SVMs need feature scaling, and training is slow on very large datasets.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"The kernel trick on a number line","html":"<p>One feature, x. Class A sits at −1, 0 and 1. Class B sits at −3 and 3.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Try a single threshold</strong> (&quot;B if x &gt; t&quot;). Any t puts some A points with some B points, because the B points are on <em>both</em> sides of A. No straight cut works in one dimension.</li>\n<li><strong>Add a new feature, x².</strong> A becomes 1, 0, 1. B becomes 9, 9.</li>\n<li><strong>Now one threshold separates them:</strong> x² &gt; 5 means B. The widest-margin cut sits halfway between the closest points, A at 1 and B at 9.</li>\n<li><strong>Map it back.</strong> x² &gt; 5 means |x| &gt; 2.24: a &quot;middle versus outside&quot; rule, which is curved in the original space.</li>\n<li><strong>The kernel trick</strong> lets an SVM work as if it had added such features (even infinitely many, for the RBF kernel) without ever computing them, by working only with similarities between points.</li>\n<li><strong>The settings.</strong> C sets how much misclassification is tolerated (small C: wider margin, more errors allowed). For the RBF kernel, gamma sets how local each point&#39;s influence is (large gamma: wiggly boundary, a risk of overfitting).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Data that is not separable in its original features can become separable after a transformation. Kernels do that transformation implicitly.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-margin-and-support-vectors","text":"Margin and support vectors","level":3},{"id":"h-soft-margin-and-c","text":"Soft margin and C","level":3},{"id":"h-kernels","text":"Kernels","level":3},{"id":"h-practical-notes","text":"Practical notes","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Find the hyperplane maximizing margin; support vectors define it. Soft margin uses <code>C</code> (high C = less tolerance for errors, risk overfitting).</li>\n<li><strong>Kernels:</strong> linear, polynomial, RBF (<code>gamma</code> controls reach: high gamma = overfitting).</li>\n<li>Needs <strong>feature scaling</strong>. Scales poorly beyond ~100k rows. No native probabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-margin-and-support-vectors\">Margin and support vectors</h3>\n<p>An SVM chooses the hyperplane <code>w·x + b = 0</code> that <strong>maximises the margin</strong> (the distance to the nearest points of each class). Only the <strong>support vectors</strong>, the points on or inside the margin, determine the solution; other points can be deleted without changing it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-soft-margin-and-c\">Soft margin and <code>C</code></h3>\n<p>Real data overlaps, so violations are allowed at a cost: minimise <code>½‖w‖² + C Σ slack</code>. <strong>Large <code>C</code></strong>: heavy penalty for errors, narrow margin, risk of <strong>overfitting</strong>. <strong>Small <code>C</code></strong>: wider margin, more tolerance, risk of underfitting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-kernels\">Kernels</h3>\n<p>The <strong>kernel trick</strong> computes similarities as if the data were mapped to a high-dimensional space without doing the mapping. Common kernels: <strong>linear</strong>, <strong>polynomial</strong>, and <strong>RBF</strong> (Gaussian) <code>exp(−γ‖x − x′‖²)</code>. <strong><code>gamma</code></strong> controls how far each point&#39;s influence reaches: a <strong>large gamma</strong> gives tiny, wiggly regions around points (<strong>overfitting</strong>); a small gamma gives smooth, nearly linear boundaries.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.svm <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SVC\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StandardScaler\nclf = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), SVC(kernel=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rbf&quot;</span>, C=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.0</span>, gamma=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;scale&quot;</span>, probability=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)).fit(X_tr, y_tr)</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-practical-notes\">Practical notes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scale features</strong>: the kernel depends on distances, so large-unit features dominate.</li>\n<li>Training scales roughly between O(n²) and O(n³): impractical beyond about 100k rows for kernel SVMs (use <code>LinearSVC</code> or SGD for large sparse data such as TF-IDF text).</li>\n<li>No native probabilities (<code>probability=True</code> adds an expensive calibration step).</li>\n<li>Good for small to medium, high-dimensional data (text with a linear kernel). On typical tabular data, gradient boosting usually wins.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-svm~1","q":"An RBF SVM scores 100% on training data and 70% on validation. Which hyperparameters do you adjust?","a":"<p>High training and low validation accuracy means overfitting. A large <code>gamma</code> makes the decision boundary hug individual points and a large <code>C</code> punishes every training error; reduce both (search on a log scale with cross-validation), make sure features are scaled, and consider a linear kernel or more data.</p>\n","s":"Lower <code>gamma</code> and/or lower <code>C</code> to regularise."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>Margin by hand.</strong> Class A points at (1, 1) and (2, 1); class B points at (4, 1) and (5, 1). The best separating line is the vertical line x = 3, halfway between the closest points (2, 1) and (4, 1). Those two are the support vectors. The distance from the line to each is 1, so the margin width is 2. Moving the point (1, 1) or (5, 1) does not change anything; only the support vectors matter. Now add an outlier of class B at (1.5, 1): no line separates the classes, so a soft margin must tolerate a mistake. A large C bends the boundary to fit the outlier (overfit); a small C ignores it.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-svm~ca0_1","q":"What are support vectors?","a":"<p>The training points closest to the boundary (on or inside the margin); only they determine the hyperplane.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-svm~ca0_2","q":"Effect of large C versus small C?","a":"<p>Large C: few violations allowed, narrow margin, risk of overfitting. Small C: wider margin, more tolerance, risk of underfitting.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-svm~ca0_3","q":"Why scale features before an RBF SVM?","a":"<p>The kernel is based on distances; a large-scale feature would dominate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-svm~ta1_1","q":"<p>Class A is at x = −2 and 2; class B is at x = 0 and 0.5. Can one threshold on x separate them? Can one threshold on x²? Which points would be the support vectors?</p>\n","a":"<p>On x: no, because B lies between the two A points. On x²: A becomes 4 and 4, B becomes 0 and 0.25, so any threshold between 0.25 and 4 works; the widest margin is at the midpoint, 2.125. The support vectors are the closest points from each class in that space: the B point at 0.5 (x² = 0.25) and the A points (x² = 4).</p>\n"}]},"m7-knn":{"id":"m7-knn","slug":"k-nearest-neighbours","m":7,"title":"K-nearest neighbours","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-core-concepts"],"tags":["machine-learning","classification","knn"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"K-nearest neighbours: prediction by voting among the closest points, distance metrics, choosing k, the need for scaling, and its costs.","keyTerms":["k-nearest neighbours","lazy learner","distance metrics","choice of k","feature scaling","curse of dimensionality"],"interviewQs":["How does KNN work and what is the effect of k?","Why does KNN need feature scaling?","What are the drawbacks of KNN?"],"mistakes":["Forgetting to scale features","Using KNN in very high dimensions","Choosing k = 1 for noisy data"],"practice":"Vary k from 1 to 50 and plot validation accuracy; find the sweet spot.","related":["m7-svm","m9-clustering","m1-scratch-ml"],"points":"- Lazy learner: predict via majority/mean of k closest points. Distance metrics: Euclidean, Manhattan, cosine.\n- Choose k by CV (small k = high variance, large k = high bias). Needs scaling. Slow at inference; suffers in high dimensions."},"intuitive":"<p>To guess what a new person likes, ask the few people who live closest to them and go with what most of them say. That is KNN. There is nothing to &quot;train&quot;: it just remembers everyone. If you ask only one neighbour (k = 1) you copy their quirks; if you ask the whole town (k huge) you just get the average opinion. And &quot;closest&quot; only makes sense if distances are fair, so all measures must be on comparable scales.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> KNN is the simplest non-parametric model and a good way to discuss bias-variance and distance.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Vote among the k nearest points; choose k by cross-validation; scale features.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;K-nearest neighbours predicts by looking at the k most similar training points and voting, or averaging for regression. There is no training, only storing the data. Similarity is a distance, usually Euclidean, so features must be scaled. Small k gives a jagged boundary and overfits; large k smooths too much. Choose k by cross-validation, often odd to avoid ties. Prediction is slow on big data because it compares against everything, and in high dimensions distances become similar for all points, the curse of dimensionality. The catch: KNN is a good baseline, and it is sensitive to irrelevant features and unscaled units.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"The choice of k changes the answer","html":"<p>Customers on one feature (monthly spend). Labels: S = stays, L = leaves. Query: a new customer who spends 50.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Customer</th><th>Spend</th><th>Label</th><th>Distance to 50</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>48</td><td>L</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>55</td><td>S</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>44</td><td>S</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>58</td><td>S</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>40</td><td>L</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>63</td><td>L</td><td>13</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sort by distance:</strong> 1 (L), 2 (S), 3 (S), 4 (S), 5 (L), 6 (L).</li>\n<li><strong>k = 1:</strong> the nearest is customer 1, so predict <strong>L</strong>. One neighbour is one noisy opinion.</li>\n<li><strong>k = 3:</strong> L, S, S, so predict <strong>S</strong> (2 votes to 1).</li>\n<li><strong>k = 5:</strong> L, S, S, S, L, so predict <strong>S</strong> (3 to 2).</li>\n<li><strong>Weighted voting</strong> (weight = 1 / distance) for k = 3: L gets 1/2 = 0.5; S gets 1/5 + 1/6 ≈ 0.37. Predict <strong>L</strong>: the closest neighbour counts most.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose k by cross-validation</strong>, often odd for two classes to avoid ties. Small k follows noise (variance); large k blurs real local patterns (bias).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> KNN has no training step: the decision is made at query time, and k and the weighting decide it. With more than one feature, scale them first, or the feature with the largest units decides alone.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-how-it-works","text":"How it works","level":3},{"id":"h-choosing-k-bias-and-variance","text":"Choosing k: bias and variance","level":3},{"id":"h-why-scaling-matters","text":"Why scaling matters","level":3},{"id":"h-weaknesses","text":"Weaknesses","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Lazy learner: predict via majority/mean of k closest points. Distance metrics: Euclidean, Manhattan, cosine.</li>\n<li>Choose k by CV (small k = high variance, large k = high bias). <strong>Needs scaling.</strong> Slow at inference; suffers in high dimensions.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-how-it-works\">How it works</h3>\n<p>For a new point: compute the distance to every training point, take the <code>k</code> nearest, then <strong>classify by majority vote</strong> (or average for regression, optionally weighted by 1/distance). It is a <strong>lazy learner</strong>: no training phase; all the work is at prediction time, O(n·d) per query with brute force (kd-trees and approximate nearest-neighbour indexes speed this up).</p>\n<p><strong>Distances:</strong> Euclidean (default), Manhattan (robust to outliers), cosine (for text and embeddings), Hamming for categories.</p>\n<p>Click to add points and move the query point to see how <code>k</code> changes the decision:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"knn","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-choosing-k-bias-and-variance\">Choosing k: bias and variance</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small k</strong> (1): the boundary is jagged and follows noise: <strong>high variance</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Large k</strong>: the boundary is smooth and may miss local structure: <strong>high bias</strong> (as k approaches n, it predicts the majority class).</li>\n<li>Choose <code>k</code> by cross-validation; use an <strong>odd k</strong> for binary problems to avoid ties.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-why-scaling-matters\">Why scaling matters</h3>\n<p>Distance is dominated by features with large numeric ranges. If income is in rupees (tens of thousands) and age in years, age is irrelevant to the distance. <strong>Standardise or min-max scale</strong> first (and fit the scaler on training data only).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-weaknesses\">Weaknesses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Slow and memory-hungry at prediction time.</li>\n<li>Suffers from the <strong>curse of dimensionality</strong>: in many dimensions all points are nearly equidistant, so &quot;nearest&quot; loses meaning. Reduce dimensions (PCA) or select features.</li>\n<li>Sensitive to irrelevant features and to class imbalance (majority votes favour the big class).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.neighbors <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> KNeighborsClassifier\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StandardScaler\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> GridSearchCV\npipe = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier())\nGridSearchCV(pipe, {<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kneighborsclassifier__n_neighbors&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">21</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">51</span>]}, cv=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>).fit(X, y)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-knn~1","q":"Your KNN model with k = 1 has 100% training accuracy. Is that impressive?","a":"<p>With k = 1 every training point&#39;s nearest neighbour is itself (distance zero), so training accuracy is always 100% and says nothing about generalisation. Evaluate on validation data or with cross-validation, and expect k = 1 to overfit noisy data.</p>\n","s":"No: each training point is its own nearest neighbour, so training accuracy is trivially 100%."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>Distance by hand.</strong> Predict whether a new customer, age 30 and income 50 (k), will buy. Neighbours: A (age 28, income 52, bought), B (age 45, income 50, no), C (age 31, income 48, bought). Unscaled Euclidean distances: A is √(2²+2²)=2.8, B is 15, C is √(1²+2²)=2.2. The three nearest are C, A, B; votes: bought, bought, no, so predict &quot;buy&quot;. Now change income to dollars (50,000 vs 52,000): income differences become thousands and swamp age completely, so the nearest neighbour is chosen by income only. That is why scaling is mandatory.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-knn~ca0_1","q":"Why does KNN need scaling?","a":"<p>It uses distance, so a feature with a larger numeric range dominates the neighbours.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-knn~ca0_2","q":"How does k affect bias and variance?","a":"<p>Small k: low bias, high variance (jagged). Large k: higher bias, lower variance (smooth).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-knn~ca0_3","q":"Why does KNN struggle in 1,000 dimensions?","a":"<p>Distances concentrate, so nearest and farthest points look similar and neighbours stop being meaningful.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-knn~ta1_1","q":"<p>Two features: age in years and income in rupees. Customer P is (30, 50,000) and Q is (60, 51,000). Your query is (31, 60,000). Which is nearer without scaling, and why is that a problem?</p>\n","a":"<p>Unscaled distance to P = √(1² + 10,000²) ≈ 10,000. To Q = √(29² + 9,000²) ≈ 9,000. So Q is &quot;nearer&quot;, although Q is 29 years older and P is nearly the same age. Income, measured in large numbers, swamps age completely. Standardise both features (subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation) so that each contributes on a comparable scale.</p>\n"}]},"m7-naive-bayes":{"id":"m7-naive-bayes","slug":"naive-bayes","m":7,"title":"Naive Bayes","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-bayes"],"tags":["machine-learning","classification","probabilistic"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Naive Bayes classifiers: Bayes' rule with conditional independence, a worked spam example with Laplace smoothing, variants, and when they work well.","keyTerms":["Naive Bayes","conditional independence","Laplace smoothing","Gaussian NB","multinomial NB","text classification"],"interviewQs":["Why is it called naive?","What is Laplace smoothing and why is it needed?","When would you use Naive Bayes?"],"mistakes":["Forgetting smoothing so unseen words zero out the product","Trusting the probability outputs","Applying Gaussian NB to heavily non-Gaussian features"],"practice":"Classify an email with two words as spam or ham by hand using the counts in the worked example.","related":["m5-bayes","m10-nlp"],"points":"- Applies Bayes with conditional independence assumption: P(y|x) proportional to P(y) * product P(x_i|y). Variants: Gaussian, Multinomial (text counts), Bernoulli.\n- Fast, good for text, works with little data; independence is unrealistic but often fine. Use Laplace smoothing for zero counts."},"intuitive":"<p>Naive Bayes is a detective who adds up clues. For each suspect (class), it asks &quot;how likely is this kind of clue among this kind of email?&quot; and multiplies the clue likelihoods together, pretending the clues do not influence each other (that is the &quot;naive&quot; part). The suspect with the highest score wins. It is surprisingly good for text, because even if words are not really independent, the combined vote still points the right way.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A fast, strong baseline for text, and a clean application of Bayes&#39; rule.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Multiply per-feature likelihoods by the prior, assume independence, and smooth the counts.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Naive Bayes applies Bayes&#39; theorem to classify: pick the class with the highest prior times the product of the feature likelihoods. The naive part is assuming features are independent given the class, which is rarely true, yet it often works well, especially for text. It is fast to train, needs little data, and handles many features. For word counts we use multinomial Naive Bayes, and Laplace smoothing adds one to every count so that a word never seen in a class does not make the whole probability zero. The catch: the probabilities it outputs are poorly calibrated, so it ranks well but its confidence is not trustworthy.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Why naive Bayes needs smoothing","html":"<p>A spam filter has learned from 100 ham (normal) emails and 100 spam emails. The word &quot;crypto&quot; appeared in 30 spam emails and in <strong>0</strong> ham emails.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Raw estimate:</strong> P(&quot;crypto&quot; | ham) = 0 / 100 = <strong>0</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The problem.</strong> Naive Bayes multiplies the probabilities of every word. A single zero makes the whole product for ham zero, so <em>any</em> email containing &quot;crypto&quot; can never be classified as ham, however strongly the other words say ham, even if it is your colleague&#39;s article about crypto regulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Laplace (add-one) smoothing.</strong> Pretend every word was seen once more in each class: P(word | class) = (count + 1) / (documents + 2) for a present/absent word. For ham: (0 + 1) / (100 + 2) ≈ <strong>0.0098</strong>. Small, but not impossible.</li>\n<li><strong>For spam:</strong> (30 + 1) / (100 + 2) ≈ <strong>0.30</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Its effect.</strong> &quot;crypto&quot; now makes spam about 30 times more likely than ham (0.30 / 0.0098), a strong signal, but other words can still outweigh it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Multiplying probabilities is fragile: one unseen word can veto everything. Smoothing keeps every probability slightly above zero. It also explains why naive Bayes probabilities are poorly calibrated even when its rankings are good.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-model","text":"The model","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-example-spam-vs-ham","text":"Worked example: spam vs ham","level":3},{"id":"h-laplace-additive-smoothing","text":"Laplace (additive) smoothing","level":3},{"id":"h-variants","text":"Variants","level":3},{"id":"h-strengths-and-limits","text":"Strengths and limits","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Applies Bayes with conditional independence assumption: P(y|x) proportional to P(y) * product P(x_i|y). Variants: Gaussian, Multinomial (text counts), Bernoulli.</li>\n<li>Fast, good for text, works with little data; independence is unrealistic but often fine. Use Laplace smoothing for zero counts.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-model\">The model</h3>\n<p>By Bayes: <code>P(y | x₁…x_d) ∝ P(y) · Π P(xᵢ | y)</code>. The <strong>naive assumption</strong> is that features are conditionally independent given the class. That makes the model need only per-feature counts per class, so training is a single pass and works with little data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-example-spam-vs-ham\">Worked example: spam vs ham</h3>\n<p>Training data: 4 spam emails and 6 ham emails. The word &quot;free&quot; appears in 3 of the spam and 1 of the ham; &quot;offer&quot; appears in 2 spam and 1 ham.</p>\n<p>For an email containing both &quot;free&quot; and &quot;offer&quot;:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">spam score = P(spam) · P(free|spam) · P(offer|spam) = 0.4 × (3/4) × (2/4) = 0.150\nham  score = P(ham)  · P(free|ham)  · P(offer|ham)  = 0.6 × (1/6) × (1/6) = 0.0167\nP(spam | free, offer) = 0.150 / (0.150 + 0.0167) = 0.90</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-laplace-additive-smoothing\">Laplace (additive) smoothing</h3>\n<p>If a word never appeared in spam during training, <code>P(word | spam) = 0</code> would multiply the whole score to zero. <strong>Laplace smoothing</strong> adds 1 to each count (and the vocabulary or category size to the denominator): <code>P(word | class) = (count + 1) / (N_class + 2)</code> for the binary &quot;present in email&quot; model. A never-seen-in-spam word then gets (0 + 1)/(4 + 2) = 1/6 rather than 0.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-variants\">Variants</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Features</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Gaussian NB</strong></td><td>continuous, modelled per class as a normal</td><td>simple numeric data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multinomial NB</strong></td><td>word counts or TF-IDF</td><td>text classification, document topics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bernoulli NB</strong></td><td>binary word presence</td><td>short texts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.feature_extraction.text <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> TfidfVectorizer\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.naive_bayes <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> MultinomialNB\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\nspam_clf = make_pipeline(TfidfVectorizer(), MultinomialNB(alpha=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.0</span>)).fit(texts_train, y_train)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># alpha = smoothing</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-strengths-and-limits\">Strengths and limits</h3>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> very fast, works with tiny data, handles many features, a strong text baseline, naturally handles missing features (just skip them). <strong>Cons:</strong> the independence assumption is unrealistic, so the <strong>probability outputs are poorly calibrated</strong> (overconfident) even when the ranking is good; it cannot learn feature interactions. It is a <strong>generative</strong> model (models P(x | y)); logistic regression is the discriminative counterpart and usually wins given enough data.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-naive-bayes~1","q":"Naive Bayes assumes conditional independence, which is clearly false for words. Why does it still work?","a":"<p>What matters for classification is which class gets the highest score, not the exact probability. Correlated features over-count evidence, making the probabilities extreme, but the <strong>argmax</strong> often stays correct. Also the model has low variance (few parameters), which is valuable with limited data. If calibrated probabilities are needed, recalibrate (Platt or isotonic), or use logistic regression.</p>\n","s":"Classification only needs the correct ranking of classes, which survives moderate dependence; errors in the probabilities often cancel."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>Spam by hand.</strong> Prior: 40% spam, 60% ham. Word &quot;free&quot; appears in 50% of spam and 5% of ham; &quot;meeting&quot; in 5% of spam and 40% of ham. For a message containing &quot;free&quot; only: spam score 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.20, ham score 0.6 × 0.05 = 0.03. Normalising gives P(spam) = 0.20 / 0.23 ≈ 87%. Add &quot;meeting&quot;: spam 0.20 × 0.05 = 0.01, ham 0.03 × 0.40 = 0.012, so P(spam) ≈ 45%. Each word multiplies the odds; the independence assumption is what makes this simple multiplication valid.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-naive-bayes~ca0_1","q":"What is the &#39;naive&#39; assumption?","a":"<p>Features are conditionally independent given the class, so likelihoods multiply.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-naive-bayes~ca0_2","q":"Why use Laplace smoothing?","a":"<p>A word unseen in a class would get probability 0 and zero out the whole product; adding a pseudo-count avoids that.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-naive-bayes~ca0_3","q":"Naive Bayes is accurate at ranking but its probabilities are off. Fix?","a":"<p>Calibrate the outputs, for example with Platt scaling or isotonic regression.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-naive-bayes~ta1_1","q":"<p>With add-one smoothing on the same 100 ham and 100 spam emails, the word &quot;meeting&quot; appears in 40 ham emails and 2 spam emails. Compute P(&quot;meeting&quot; | ham) and P(&quot;meeting&quot; | spam), and the factor by which &quot;meeting&quot; shifts the odds.</p>\n","a":"<p>P(meeting | ham) = (40 + 1) / 102 ≈ 0.40. P(meeting | spam) = (2 + 1) / 102 ≈ 0.029. The ratio ham to spam is about 0.40 / 0.029 ≈ 14, so seeing &quot;meeting&quot; multiplies the odds in favour of ham by about 14.</p>\n"}]},"m7-ensembling":{"id":"m7-ensembling","slug":"ensembling-and-the-algorithm-selection-guide","m":7,"title":"Ensembling and the algorithm selection guide","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-gradient-boosting"],"tags":["machine-learning","ensembles","model-selection"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Ensembling methods and why diversity matters, how to stack correctly with out-of-fold predictions, and a practical guide for choosing an algorithm for a given situation.","keyTerms":["bagging","boosting","stacking","blending","voting","diversity","out-of-fold predictions","algorithm selection"],"interviewQs":["What is stacking and how do you avoid leakage when doing it?","Why do ensembles work?","Which algorithm would you pick for interpretable credit scoring? For best accuracy on tabular data?"],"mistakes":["Stacking with in-sample predictions (leakage)","Ensembling very similar models and expecting gains","Choosing the algorithm before understanding the constraints"],"practice":"Build a voting ensemble of logistic regression, random forest and gradient boosting, and compare its cross-validated AUC with each member.","related":["m7-random-forest","m7-gradient-boosting","m13-problem-framing"],"points":"- Bagging, boosting, stacking (meta-model on out-of-fold predictions), blending, voting.\n- Diversity among models is what makes ensembles work.\n\nSituation - Suggested approach\nNeed interpretability / regulated (credit) - Logistic/linear regression, shallow trees, scorecards, SHAP\nTabular data, best accuracy - LightGBM/XGBoost/CatBoost\nSmall data - Regularized linear models, RF, Naive Bayes\nText - TF-IDF + logistic regression/linear SVM, then transformers\nImages - CNNs / pre-trained vision models\nVery high cardinality categoricals - CatBoost, target encoding (out-of-fold), embeddings\nNeed probabilities - Logistic regression, calibrated tree models\nNeeds quick baseline - Logistic/linear regression, DummyClassifier"},"intuitive":"<p>Ask one friend for advice and you get one opinion. Ask several friends who think differently and combine their answers, and you usually do better, because their mistakes do not all point the same way. That is an ensemble. You can average them (bagging), let each one correct the last one&#39;s mistakes (boosting), or train a final &quot;manager&quot; model that learns how much to trust each friend (stacking). The key word is <em>different</em>: five copies of the same friend add nothing.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Ensembles win most structured-data competitions and power most production systems.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Combine diverse models; for stacking, feed the manager only out-of-fold predictions.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Ensembling combines models for better predictions. Bagging trains parallel models on bootstrap samples and averages them, cutting variance, as in random forest. Boosting trains sequentially, cutting bias. Stacking trains a meta-model on the predictions of several different base models, using out-of-fold predictions to avoid leakage. Simple averaging of different models also works well. For choosing an algorithm: start with a simple baseline like logistic regression, use gradient boosting for tabular data, linear models when interpretability matters, KNN or SVM for small data, and deep learning for images, text and audio. The catch: ensembles cost more to serve and explain, so use them only if the gain justifies it.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Three independent models vote","html":"<p>Three models are each 70% accurate, and their mistakes are independent. The ensemble takes a majority vote.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The majority is right if at least two of the three are right.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>All three right:</strong> 0.7³ = <strong>0.343</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exactly two right:</strong> there are 3 ways to choose which one is wrong: 3 × 0.7² × 0.3 = <strong>0.441</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ensemble accuracy:</strong> 0.343 + 0.441 = <strong>0.784</strong>, against 0.70 for any single model.</li>\n<li><strong>With five such models:</strong> P(at least 3 of 5 right) ≈ <strong>0.837</strong>. More independent voters, better accuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>The catch.</strong> If the models make the <em>same</em> mistakes (all trained on the same features with the same method), voting changes nothing. Gains come from diversity: different algorithms, features or data samples.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Ensembles work by cancelling uncorrelated errors. That is why random forests randomise their trees and why stacking combines different kinds of model.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-7-3-9","text":"7.3.9 Ensembling","level":2},{"id":"c-7-3-10","text":"7.3.10 Algorithm selection guide","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-families","text":"The families","level":3},{"id":"h-stacking-without-leakage","text":"Stacking without leakage","level":3},{"id":"h-algorithm-selection-guide-with-reasons","text":"Algorithm selection guide, with reasons","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-7-3-9\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.3.9</span> Ensembling</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Bagging, boosting, stacking (meta-model on out-of-fold predictions), blending, voting.</strong></li>\n<li>Diversity among models is what makes ensembles work.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-7-3-10\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.3.10</span> Algorithm selection guide</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Suggested approach</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Need interpretability / regulated (credit)</td><td>Logistic/linear regression, shallow trees, scorecards, SHAP</td></tr><tr><td>Tabular data, best accuracy</td><td>LightGBM/XGBoost/CatBoost</td></tr><tr><td>Small data</td><td>Regularized linear models, RF, Naive Bayes</td></tr><tr><td>Text</td><td>TF-IDF + logistic regression/linear SVM, then transformers</td></tr><tr><td>Images</td><td>CNNs / pre-trained vision models</td></tr><tr><td>Very high cardinality categoricals</td><td>CatBoost, target encoding (out-of-fold), embeddings</td></tr><tr><td>Need probabilities</td><td>Logistic regression, calibrated tree models</td></tr><tr><td>Needs quick baseline</td><td>Logistic/linear regression, DummyClassifier</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-families\">The families</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Idea</th><th>Reduces</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Bagging</strong></td><td>average models trained on bootstrap samples (random forest)</td><td>variance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Boosting</strong></td><td>sequentially add models that fix previous errors (GBM)</td><td>bias (and some variance)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Voting</strong></td><td>average probabilities (soft) or vote labels (hard) of different model types</td><td>variance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stacking</strong></td><td>a meta-model learns to combine base-model predictions</td><td>both, if base models differ</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Blending</strong></td><td>like stacking but the meta-model trains on a held-out split rather than out-of-fold predictions</td><td>simpler, uses less data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Why ensembles work:</strong> if each model has error variance σ² and their errors have correlation ρ, the average has variance <code>ρσ² + (1 − ρ)σ²/M</code>. The less correlated the models (different algorithms, feature sets, seeds, data views), the larger the gain.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-stacking-without-leakage\">Stacking without leakage</h3>\n<p>If the meta-model is trained on base-model predictions for the <em>same rows those models were trained on</em>, the base predictions are over-optimistic (they have seen the labels), and the meta-model learns to trust them too much. Use <strong>out-of-fold (OOF)</strong> predictions: for each fold, train on the other folds and predict the held-out fold; concatenate the predictions to get a clean &quot;as if unseen&quot; prediction for every training row.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StackingClassifier, RandomForestClassifier, GradientBoostingClassifier\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> LogisticRegression\n\nstack = StackingClassifier(\n    estimators=[(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;lr&quot;</span>, LogisticRegression(max_iter=<span class=\"hljs-number\">2000</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rf&quot;</span>, RandomForestClassifier(<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;gb&quot;</span>, GradientBoostingClassifier())],\n    final_estimator=LogisticRegression(),            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># the meta-model; kept simple</span>\n    cv=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,                                            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># base predictions are OUT-OF-FOLD</span>\n    stack_method=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;predict_proba&quot;</span>)\nstack.fit(X_train, y_train)</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-algorithm-selection-guide-with-reasons\">Algorithm selection guide, with reasons</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Suggested approach</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interpretability or regulation (credit)</td><td>logistic / linear regression, shallow trees, scorecards, SHAP for explanations</td><td>coefficients and rules can be audited</td></tr><tr><td>Tabular data, best accuracy</td><td>LightGBM / XGBoost / CatBoost</td><td>handles non-linearity and interactions, robust, fast</td></tr><tr><td>Small data</td><td>regularised linear models, random forest, Naive Bayes</td><td>low variance, few parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Text</td><td>TF-IDF + logistic regression / linear SVM first, then transformers</td><td>strong, cheap baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Images</td><td>CNNs or pre-trained vision models</td><td>exploit spatial structure</td></tr><tr><td>Very high cardinality categoricals</td><td>CatBoost, out-of-fold target encoding, embeddings</td><td>avoid one-hot explosion without leakage</td></tr><tr><td>Need well-calibrated probabilities</td><td>logistic regression, or calibrate tree models</td><td>trees and NB are often miscalibrated</td></tr><tr><td>Quick baseline</td><td>logistic/linear regression, <code>DummyClassifier</code></td><td>sets the bar to beat</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>How to answer &quot;which algorithm?&quot; in an interview:</strong> ask about the data size, the feature types, the need for interpretability and latency, and the cost of errors; propose a baseline first, then a stronger model; say how you would compare them (cross-validated metric aligned with business cost).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-ensembling~1","q":"You have 5,000 rows, 30 mostly numeric features, a regulator who requires explainable decisions, and a binary target. Which model and why?","a":"<p>Start with <strong>regularised logistic regression</strong> (possibly scorecard-style with WoE binning): interpretable coefficients, stable on 5,000 rows, easy to document. Compare with a constrained <strong>gradient boosting</strong> model (monotonic constraints, shallow trees) and explain it with <strong>SHAP</strong>; if the lift over logistic regression is small, prefer the simpler model. Validate with stratified cross-validation and report metrics tied to the business cost (for example KS, AUC, and expected loss at the approval threshold).</p>\n","s":"Regularised logistic regression (maybe with WoE binning), compared against a shallow gradient-boosted model with SHAP."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-ensembling~ca0_1","q":"Why does stacking use out-of-fold predictions for the meta-model?","a":"<p>In-sample base predictions are overly good, so the meta-model would trust them too much; out-of-fold predictions mimic unseen data and prevent leakage.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-ensembling~ca0_2","q":"Best default model for a tabular business dataset?","a":"<p>Start with a simple baseline, then gradient boosting (XGBoost or LightGBM), which usually wins on tabular data.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-ensembling~ca0_3","q":"When would you skip an ensemble?","a":"<p>When latency, cost, or explainability matters more than a small accuracy gain.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-ensembling~ta1_1","q":"<p>Model A is 80% accurate and models B and C are each 60% accurate, all with independent errors. Is a majority vote of the three better than A alone?</p>\n","a":"<p>The vote is right when at least two are right. All three right: 0.8 × 0.6 × 0.6 = 0.288. A and B right, C wrong: 0.8 × 0.6 × 0.4 = 0.192. A and C right, B wrong: 0.192. B and C right, A wrong: 0.2 × 0.6 × 0.6 = 0.072. Total = 0.744, which is worse than A&#39;s 0.80. Weak voters can outvote a strong model. Weight the votes, use stacking, or just use A.</p>\n"}]},"m7-optimization":{"id":"m7-optimization","slug":"optimisation-gradient-descent-and-friends","m":7,"title":"Optimisation: gradient descent and friends","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-linear-regression"],"tags":["machine-learning","optimization","gradient-descent"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Gradient descent and its variants, how the learning rate and feature scaling affect convergence, momentum and Adam, and convex vs non-convex problems.","keyTerms":["gradient descent","learning rate","stochastic and mini-batch","momentum","RMSprop","Adam","convex vs non-convex","feature scaling"],"interviewQs":["Explain gradient descent and the role of the learning rate.","Batch vs stochastic vs mini-batch gradient descent?","Why does feature scaling speed up convergence?","Is the loss of linear regression convex? Of a neural network?"],"mistakes":["Using a learning rate that is too large and calling it \"no convergence\"","Forgetting to scale features","Treating local minima as the main deep-learning problem"],"practice":"Minimise f(x) = x² with learning rates 0.1, 0.9 and 1.1 and describe each trajectory.","related":["m1-scratch-ml","m10-dl-fundamentals","m5-other"],"points":"- Gradient descent: w := w - lr * dL/dw. Batch (all data), SGD (one sample), mini-batch (most common).\n- Learning rate: too high diverges, too low slow; schedules/decay.\n- Momentum, RMSprop, Adam (adaptive; default in deep learning).\n- Convex vs non-convex: linear/logistic regression are convex (global minimum); neural nets are not.\n- Feature scaling speeds convergence."},"intuitive":"<p>Gradient descent is walking downhill in fog. You cannot see the valley, but you can feel the slope under your feet, so you take a step downhill and check again. The length of your step is the learning rate: too small and it takes forever; too big and you leap across the valley and end up higher. Mini-batch versions estimate the slope from a handful of examples instead of all of them, which is much cheaper. Momentum is like a heavy ball rolling: it builds up speed in a consistent direction and smooths out wiggles.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> It is how almost every modern model is trained, and learning-rate questions are common.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Step against the gradient; tune the learning rate; scale your features.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Training means minimising a loss. Gradient descent computes the slope of the loss with respect to each weight and steps downhill: new weight equals old weight minus learning rate times gradient. Too large a learning rate overshoots or diverges; too small is slow. Batch gradient descent uses all data per step; stochastic uses one example, noisy but fast; mini-batch is the practical middle. Momentum smooths the steps, and Adam adapts a learning rate per weight, which is the usual default for deep learning. Convex losses, like linear and logistic regression, have one global minimum; neural networks have many local ones, but good optima are usually found. The catch: feature scaling speeds up convergence a lot.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Three learning rates on the simplest loss","html":"<p>Minimise f(w) = w², whose slope (gradient) is 2w, starting from w = 1. Update: w ← w − η × 2w.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>η = 0.1</th><th>η = 0.4</th><th>η = 1.1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>start</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.8</td><td>0.2</td><td>−1.2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.64</td><td>0.04</td><td>1.44</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.512</td><td>0.008</td><td>−1.728</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>η = 0.1, too cautious.</strong> Each step keeps 80% of w. It gets there, slowly.</li>\n<li><strong>η = 0.4, well chosen.</strong> Each step keeps 20%. Close to the minimum in three steps.</li>\n<li><strong>η = 1.1, too big.</strong> Each step multiplies w by −1.2: it jumps over the minimum and lands further away each time. The loss <em>grows</em>. This is what &quot;the loss exploded to NaN&quot; looks like.</li>\n<li><strong>Why features need scaling.</strong> With two weights whose features have very different scales, one direction is steep and the other is flat. A single learning rate is then too big for one and too small for the other, and descent zig-zags. Standardising the features makes the bowl round.</li>\n<li><strong>Adam and momentum</strong> adapt the step for each weight and smooth the path, which is why they are the default for neural networks.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The learning rate is the most important setting in gradient-based training: too small wastes time, too large diverges.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-update-and-the-learning-rate","text":"The update and the learning rate","level":3},{"id":"h-batch-stochastic-mini-batch","text":"Batch, stochastic, mini-batch","level":3},{"id":"h-momentum-and-adaptive-methods","text":"Momentum and adaptive methods","level":3},{"id":"h-convex-vs-non-convex","text":"Convex vs non-convex","level":3},{"id":"h-why-feature-scaling-speeds-convergence","text":"Why feature scaling speeds convergence","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Gradient descent:</strong> <code>w := w - lr * dL/dw</code>. <strong>Batch</strong> (all data), <strong>SGD</strong> (one sample), <strong>mini-batch</strong> (most common).</li>\n<li><strong>Learning rate:</strong> too high diverges, too low slow; schedules/decay.</li>\n<li><strong>Momentum, RMSprop, Adam</strong> (adaptive; default in deep learning).</li>\n<li><strong>Convex vs non-convex:</strong> linear/logistic regression are convex (global minimum); neural nets are not.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature scaling speeds convergence.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-update-and-the-learning-rate\">The update and the learning rate</h3>\n<p><code>θ ← θ − η ∇L(θ)</code>. For <code>f(x) = x²</code> (gradient <code>2x</code>) starting at 5:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Learning rate η</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.1</td><td>x shrinks 20% per step: 4.0, 3.2, 2.56, 2.05, 1.64 ... smooth convergence</td></tr><tr><td>0.9</td><td>x flips sign each step and shrinks slowly: 5 → −4 → 3.2 ... oscillates but converges</td></tr><tr><td>1.0</td><td>x jumps between 5 and −5 forever</td></tr><tr><td>1.1</td><td>each step multiplies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Explore it on a 2D bowl:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"gd","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>Too small: slow and stuck on plateaus. Too large: overshoot, oscillate or diverge. Good practice: start with a sensible default, watch the <strong>training loss curve</strong>, use a <strong>learning-rate schedule</strong> (decay, cosine, warm-up) and, for adaptive optimisers, rely on the defaults.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-batch-stochastic-mini-batch\">Batch, stochastic, mini-batch</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Gradient computed from</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Batch</strong></td><td>all n samples</td><td>exact, smooth; slow per step and memory-heavy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stochastic (SGD)</strong></td><td>one sample</td><td>very cheap but noisy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mini-batch</strong></td><td>32 to 512 samples</td><td>the standard: good noise/cost balance and GPU-friendly</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The noise in SGD is not only a cost: it can help escape shallow minima and saddle points.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-momentum-and-adaptive-methods\">Momentum and adaptive methods</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Momentum:</strong> keep a running average of gradients, <code>v ← βv + ∇L; θ ← θ − ηv</code>. Accelerates along consistent directions, damps oscillation across narrow valleys.</li>\n<li><strong>RMSprop:</strong> scale each parameter&#39;s step by a running average of its recent squared gradients, so steep directions take smaller steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Adam / AdamW:</strong> momentum plus RMSprop-style scaling with bias correction; the default in deep learning (AdamW decouples weight decay). Often needs less learning-rate tuning; plain SGD with momentum sometimes generalises slightly better on vision tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-convex-vs-non-convex\">Convex vs non-convex</h3>\n<p>A <strong>convex</strong> loss has a single global minimum and no spurious local minima: linear regression, logistic regression and SVMs are convex, so gradient descent (with a suitable step) converges to the global optimum. <strong>Neural-network</strong> losses are <strong>non-convex</strong> (many saddle points, flat regions, local minima), yet in practice good solutions are found; the main difficulties are saddle points, plateaus and sharp vs flat minima rather than getting stuck at poor local minima.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-why-feature-scaling-speeds-convergence\">Why feature scaling speeds convergence</h3>\n<p>If one feature has a much larger range than another, the loss surface is a long, narrow valley: the gradient points mostly across the valley, so the optimiser zig-zags and needs a tiny learning rate. <strong>Standardising</strong> the features makes the contours closer to circles, so the gradient points toward the minimum and a larger stable learning rate can be used. Gradient-based and distance-based models need scaling; trees do not.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">gd</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">f_grad, x0, lr, steps=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span></span>):\n    x = x0\n    path = [x]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> _ <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(steps):\n        x = x - lr * f_grad(x)\n        path.append(x)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> np.array(path)\n\ngd(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> x: <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> * x, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.1</span>)[:<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>]     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [5. 4. 3.2 2.56 2.048]</span>\ngd(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> x: <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> * x, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.1</span>)[:<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>]     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [5. -6. 7.2 -8.64]   diverging</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-optimization~1","q":"Training loss goes to <code>nan</code> after a few steps. What do you check?","a":"<p><code>nan</code> almost always means numerical blow-up. Check: (1) the <strong>learning rate</strong> (reduce it by 10x); (2) <strong>feature scaling</strong> and outliers in inputs or targets; (3) <strong>exploding gradients</strong> in deep nets (use gradient clipping, proper initialisation, normalisation layers); (4) invalid operations such as <code>log(0)</code> or division by zero in a custom loss; (5) corrupt data with NaNs or infinities.</p>\n","s":"Learning rate too high (or unscaled inputs, exploding gradients, bad data); lower the rate, scale features, clip gradients."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-optimization~ca0_1","q":"Loss goes up and down wildly and then becomes NaN. First thing to try?","a":"<p>Lower the learning rate (and check for unscaled inputs or exploding gradients).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-optimization~ca0_2","q":"Why mini-batch gradient descent rather than full-batch?","a":"<p>It gives frequent, cheaper updates that fit in memory, and the noise can help escape poor regions; it also uses hardware efficiently.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-optimization~ca0_3","q":"What does Adam add to plain SGD?","a":"<p>Momentum plus per-parameter adaptive learning rates from running averages of gradients and squared gradients.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-optimization~ta1_1","q":"<p>For the same f(w) = w² starting at w = 1, what happens with η = 0.5? And with η = 1.0?</p>\n","a":"<p>η = 0.5: w ← w − 0.5 × 2w = 0, the exact minimum in one step (for this particular function). η = 1.0: w ← w − 2w = −w, so w flips between 1 and −1 forever and never converges. For this loss, any η between 0 and 1 converges and anything above 1 diverges.</p>\n"}]},"m7-tuning":{"id":"m7-tuning","slug":"hyperparameter-tuning-and-sklearn-pipelines","m":7,"title":"Hyperparameter tuning and sklearn pipelines","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-leakage"],"tags":["machine-learning","hyperparameters","sklearn","pipelines"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Hyperparameter tuning strategies and a leakage-free scikit-learn pipeline, with a line-by-line reading of the course code.","keyTerms":["grid search","random search","Bayesian optimisation","nested cross-validation","Pipeline","ColumnTransformer","Optuna","successive halving"],"interviewQs":["Grid vs random vs Bayesian search?","What is nested cross-validation and when do you need it?","Why put preprocessing inside a Pipeline?"],"mistakes":["Tuning on the test set","A huge grid over unimportant parameters","Reporting the best cross-validation score as the final estimate"],"practice":"Tune a random forest with RandomizedSearchCV inside a Pipeline and report the score on an untouched test set.","related":["m7-leakage","m7-gradient-boosting","m8-preprocessing"],"points":"- Grid search (exhaustive), random search (more efficient in high dimensions), Bayesian optimization (Optuna, Hyperopt; model-guided), successive halving.\n- Tune with nested CV or a clean validation split; avoid tuning on the test set."},"intuitive":"<p>Hyperparameters are the dials on a machine that you set before it starts (how deep trees may grow, how strong the penalty is). Tuning is turning the dials to find the best combination. Trying every combination (grid search) is thorough but wasteful; trying random combinations (random search) finds good settings faster because only a few dials really matter. Smarter methods learn from previous tries. And the golden rule: judge the final machine on data it never saw while you were turning dials.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers want to see you can tune without leaking or overfitting.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Random search in a pipeline, cross-validated, with an untouched test set at the end.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Hyperparameters are settings chosen before training, like tree depth or regularisation strength. I tune them with cross-validation: split training data into k folds, train on k minus one, score on the held-out fold, and average. Grid search tries every combination; random search samples combinations and is usually more efficient; Bayesian optimisation uses past results to choose the next try. A sklearn Pipeline chains preprocessing and the model, so that during cross-validation scaling and imputing are refit inside each fold, which prevents leakage. I tune on validation folds only and touch the test set once. The catch: tuning too much on one validation set overfits to it, so I use nested cross-validation if the final estimate matters.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Five-fold cross-validation, by numbers","html":"<p>Compare two settings of a model&#39;s depth using 5-fold cross-validation. AUC on each validation fold:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fold</th><th>depth 4</th><th>depth 10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.81</td><td>0.84</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.79</td><td>0.76</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.82</td><td>0.85</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.80</td><td>0.75</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.81</td><td>0.83</td></tr><tr><td><strong>mean</strong></td><td><strong>0.806</strong></td><td><strong>0.806</strong></td></tr><tr><td>spread (max − min)</td><td>0.03</td><td>0.10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Each fold trains on 4/5 of the training data</strong> and validates on the remaining 1/5, so every row is used for validation exactly once.</li>\n<li><strong>The means tie at 0.806.</strong> On average neither setting is better.</li>\n<li><strong>Look at the spread.</strong> Depth 10 swings between 0.75 and 0.85, so it is unstable: its result depends heavily on which rows it saw, a variance problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose depth 4:</strong> the same average with far more consistency, and a simpler model.</li>\n<li><strong>Refit depth 4 on all the training data</strong>, then score it <strong>once</strong> on the untouched test set for the number you report.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep preprocessing inside the folds.</strong> Put the scaler and imputer in a pipeline so they are refit on each fold&#39;s training part; otherwise the validation folds leak into them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Cross-validation gives you a mean and a spread. When the means are close, prefer the simpler, more stable setting.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-strategies","text":"Strategies","level":3},{"id":"h-reading-the-course-pipeline","text":"Reading the course pipeline","level":3},{"id":"h-a-compact-optuna-example","text":"A compact Optuna example","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Grid search (exhaustive), random search (more efficient in high dimensions), Bayesian optimization (Optuna, Hyperopt; model-guided), successive halving.</li>\n<li>Tune with <strong>nested CV</strong> or a clean validation split; avoid tuning on the test set.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Pipeline\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.compose <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> ColumnTransformer\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.impute <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SimpleImputer\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> RandomForestClassifier\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> RandomizedSearchCV, StratifiedKFold\n\nnum_pipe = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;imp&#x27;</span>, SimpleImputer(strategy=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;median&#x27;</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sc&#x27;</span>, StandardScaler())])\ncat_pipe = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;imp&#x27;</span>, SimpleImputer(strategy=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;most_frequent&#x27;</span>)),\n                     (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;oh&#x27;</span>, OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ignore&#x27;</span>))])\npre = ColumnTransformer([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;num&#x27;</span>, num_pipe, num_cols), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cat&#x27;</span>, cat_pipe, cat_cols)])\n\nmodel = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;pre&#x27;</span>, pre), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;clf&#x27;</span>, RandomForestClassifier(class_weight=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;balanced&#x27;</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>))])\n\nsearch = RandomizedSearchCV(\n    model,\n    {<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;clf__n_estimators&#x27;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">200</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">800</span>], <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;clf__max_depth&#x27;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>],\n     <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;clf__min_samples_leaf&#x27;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>]},\n    n_iter=<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>, scoring=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;average_precision&#x27;</span>,\n    cv=StratifiedKFold(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, shuffle=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>), n_jobs=-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>)\nsearch.fit(X_train, y_train)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-strategies\">Strategies</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Grid search</strong></td><td>every combination of listed values</td><td>exhaustive; cost multiplies with each parameter</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Random search</strong></td><td>sample combinations from distributions</td><td>usually better per unit of compute: often only a few parameters matter, and random sampling tries many distinct values of each</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bayesian optimisation</strong> (Optuna, Hyperopt)</td><td>model-guided: a surrogate predicts promising regions</td><td>efficient for expensive models</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Successive halving / Hyperband</strong></td><td>train many configurations briefly, keep the best, give them more budget</td><td>good when training can be cut short</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use <strong>nested cross-validation</strong> (an inner loop tunes, an outer loop evaluates) when you need an unbiased performance estimate on small data; otherwise a clean train/validation/test split with tuning via cross-validation on the training portion is fine. The best cross-validation score is optimistic because you selected the maximum over many trials.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-reading-the-course-pipeline\">Reading the course pipeline</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">num_pipe = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;imp&quot;</span>, SimpleImputer(strategy=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;median&quot;</span>)), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sc&quot;</span>, StandardScaler())])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># numeric: fill, then scale</span>\ncat_pipe = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;imp&quot;</span>, SimpleImputer(strategy=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;most_frequent&quot;</span>)),                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># categorical: fill, then one-hot</span>\n                     (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;oh&quot;</span>, OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ignore&quot;</span>))])                         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ignore = unseen categories at predict time are all zeros</span>\npre = ColumnTransformer([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;num&quot;</span>, num_pipe, num_cols), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cat&quot;</span>, cat_pipe, cat_cols)])           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># route columns to the right steps</span>\nmodel = Pipeline([(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pre&quot;</span>, pre), (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;clf&quot;</span>, RandomForestClassifier(class_weight=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;balanced&quot;</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>))])\n\nsearch = RandomizedSearchCV(model,\n    {<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;clf__n_estimators&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">200</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">800</span>], <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;clf__max_depth&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>], <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;clf__min_samples_leaf&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>]},\n    n_iter=<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>, scoring=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;average_precision&quot;</span>,                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># PR-AUC, sensible for an imbalanced target</span>\n    cv=StratifiedKFold(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, shuffle=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>), n_jobs=-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">42</span>)\nsearch.fit(X_train, y_train)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Key points to say aloud:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>Pipeline</code> + <code>ColumnTransformer</code></strong> guarantee that imputation, scaling and encoding are <strong>fitted inside each cross-validation fold</strong>, so no information leaks from validation rows.</li>\n<li>The double underscore <code>clf__max_depth</code> addresses a parameter of a named step.</li>\n<li><code>StratifiedKFold</code> keeps class ratios in every fold; <code>scoring</code> should match the business metric.</li>\n<li><code>n_iter=15</code> samples 15 of the 36 possible combinations: a budget instead of a grid.</li>\n<li>After the search, <code>search.best_estimator_</code> is <strong>refit on all training data</strong>; evaluate it <strong>once</strong> on the held-out test set.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(search.best_params_, search.best_score_)                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># the selected settings and their CV score (optimistic)</span>\nfinal = average_precision_score(y_test, search.predict_proba(X_test)[:, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># the honest number</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-compact-optuna-example\">A compact Optuna example</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> optuna, lightgbm <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> lgb\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> cross_val_score\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">objective</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">trial</span>):\n    params = {\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;learning_rate&quot;</span>: trial.suggest_float(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;learning_rate&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.01</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.2</span>, log=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;num_leaves&quot;</span>: trial.suggest_int(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;num_leaves&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">64</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;min_child_samples&quot;</span>: trial.suggest_int(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;min_child_samples&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;subsample&quot;</span>: trial.suggest_float(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;subsample&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.0</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;n_estimators&quot;</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>,\n    }\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> cross_val_score(lgb.LGBMClassifier(**params, subsample_freq=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, verbose=-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>), X_train, y_train, cv=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, scoring=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;roc_auc&quot;</span>).mean()\n\nstudy = optuna.create_study(direction=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;maximize&quot;</span>); study.optimize(objective, n_trials=<span class=\"hljs-number\">40</span>)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-tuning~1","q":"You ran a 500-configuration search and the best cross-validated AUC is 0.91. Your test AUC is 0.87. Is something wrong?","a":"<p>Choosing the maximum over hundreds of noisy estimates biases the winning score upward (a <strong>selection effect</strong>). A small gap like 0.91 to 0.87 is expected; a huge gap would suggest leakage or distribution shift between the training and test periods. Report the test number, reduce the search size or use nested CV, and check that the test set was used only once.</p>\n","s":"No: selecting the best of many configurations makes the CV score optimistic; the test score is the honest estimate."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-tuning~ca0_1","q":"Why put the scaler inside a Pipeline when cross-validating?","a":"<p>So it is fit only on each training fold; fitting it on all data first leaks validation information.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-tuning~ca0_2","q":"Random search versus grid search?","a":"<p>Random search covers more distinct values of each important hyperparameter for the same budget and is typically more efficient in high dimensions.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-tuning~ca0_3","q":"Time series data: which CV scheme?","a":"<p>Forward-chaining (TimeSeriesSplit): always train on the past and validate on the future.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m7-tuning~ta1_1","q":"<p>You have 12 months of data and want to tune a forecasting model with 3 time-series folds that always train on the past. Write the train and validation months for each fold.</p>\n","a":"<p>One common scheme (expanding window, one month of validation): Fold 1 trains on months 1 to 8 and validates on month 9. Fold 2 trains on months 1 to 9 and validates on month 10. Fold 3 trains on months 1 to 10 and validates on month 11. Month 12 is kept as the final test set. Never validate on a month that comes before any training month.</p>\n"}]},"m7-qa":{"id":"m7-qa","slug":"ml-q-a-and-self-check","m":7,"title":"ML Q&A and self-check","min":30,"type":"revise","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m7-gradient-boosting"],"tags":["machine-learning","interview-questions"],"qa":19,"ai":{"scope":"Rapid-fire machine learning interview questions with concise answers and a self-check for the whole of Module 7.","keyTerms":["random forest variance","feature scaling","multicollinearity","regularisation trade-off","train-serve skew","k-means","precision-recall","SHAP","fit vs transform"],"interviewQs":["Why does a model perform well in training and poorly in production?","How do you explain a black-box model?","What is the difference between fit, transform and fit_transform?"],"mistakes":["Answers that list terms without a reason","Forgetting the train-serve skew causes","Not mentioning calibration or costs"],"practice":"Answer each question aloud in under 60 seconds with one example, then reveal and compare.","related":["m16-ml-1","m16-ml-2"],"points":"Q: How does Random Forest reduce variance?\nA: Averages many decorrelated high-variance trees (bootstrap + feature subsampling).\n\nQ: Why scale features?\nA: Distance/gradient-based models (KNN, SVM, NN, regularized linear) are scale-sensitive; trees are not.\n\nQ: How to detect/handle multicollinearity?\nA: Correlation matrix, VIF; drop/merge features, PCA, regularization.\n\nQ: What is regularization strength trade-off?\nA: Higher lambda = simpler model, more bias, less variance.\n\nQ: Why does a model perform well in training and poorly in production?\nA: Leakage, train-serve skew, distribution drift, different population, feature unavailability at inference, overfitting to validation.\n\nQ: How do you pick k in K-Means?\nA: Elbow (inertia), silhouette score, gap statistic, domain needs.\n\nQ: Is accuracy always good?\nA: No; with imbalance use precision/recall/PR-AUC/F1.\n\nQ: How do trees handle missing values?\nA: Depends: XGBoost/LightGBM learn default direction; sklearn (recent versions) supports NaN in some tree models; otherwise impute.\n\nQ: Explain precision-recall tradeoff.\nA: Raising threshold increases precision, decreases recall, and vice versa.\n\nQ: How to explain a black-box model?\nA: Feature importance, permutation importance, PDP/ICE, SHAP, LIME, surrogate models.\n\nQ: Bagging vs boosting vs stacking?\nA: See 7.3.5/7.3.9.\n\nQ: Difference between parameters and hyperparameters?\nA: Parameters learned from data (weights, split points); hyperparameters set before training (depth, lr).\n\nQ: What is the difference between fit, transform, fit_transform?\nA: fit learns statistics from data; transform applies them; fit_transform does both (only on train).\n\n- Explain each algorithm in 2 minutes: intuition, loss, key hyperparameters, pros/cons.\n- Implement logistic regression and k-means from scratch.\n- For a given business problem, justify the algorithm choice."},"intuitive":"<p>This section is your ML spoken-answer drill. Most questions are one of a few shapes: compare two things (bagging versus boosting), diagnose a symptom (high train, low test score), choose a tool for a situation, or explain why something works. For each, a strong answer names the idea in a sentence, gives the reason, then states a trade-off or a trap. Practise saying them aloud with a timer. If a question exposes a gap, jump back to that algorithm and re-read its intuition before returning here.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> ML interviews are conversations; clear comparisons and diagnoses win more than formulas.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Compare, diagnose, choose, justify, each with a trade-off.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For an ML question I use a four-part answer. First, name the concept in plain words. Second, give the reason it works or fails, the mechanism. Third, a concrete example with numbers or a situation. Fourth, the trade-off: what it costs and when I would not use it. For bias and variance: too-simple models miss patterns, too-flexible ones memorise noise; for example, a depth-two tree versus a depth-thirty tree; the trade-off is controlled with regularisation and validation. If I do not know, I reason from the basics and say what I would check. The catch: long theory without an example loses the listener.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"c-7-6","text":"7.6 ML Q&amp;A (short answers)","level":2},{"id":"c-7-7","text":"7.7 Self-check (Module 7)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-extra-questions-with-model-answers","text":"Extra questions with model answers","level":3},{"id":"h-self-check-from-the-course","text":"Self-check (from the course)","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-7-6\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.6</span> ML Q&amp;A (short answers)</h2>"},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#1","src":"c","q":"How does Random Forest reduce variance?","a":"<p>Averages many decorrelated high-variance trees (bootstrap + feature subsampling).</p>\n","s":"Averaging many decorrelated, high-variance trees (bootstrap samples plus random feature subsets) reduces variance."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#2","src":"c","q":"Why scale features?","a":"<p>Distance/gradient-based models (KNN, SVM, NN, regularized linear) are scale-sensitive; trees are not.</p>\n","s":"Distance- and gradient-based models (KNN, SVM, neural nets, regularised linear models) are scale-sensitive; trees are not."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#3","src":"c","q":"How to detect/handle multicollinearity?","a":"<p>Correlation matrix, VIF; drop/merge features, PCA, regularization.</p>\n","s":"Use the correlation matrix and VIF; drop or merge features, use PCA or regularisation."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#4","src":"c","q":"What is regularization strength trade-off?","a":"<p>Higher lambda = simpler model, more bias, less variance.</p>\n","s":"Higher lambda gives a simpler model with more bias and less variance."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#5","src":"c","q":"Why does a model perform well in training and poorly in production?","a":"<p>Leakage, train-serve skew, distribution drift, different population, feature unavailability at inference, overfitting to validation.</p>\n","s":"Leakage, train-serve skew, drift, a different population, unavailable features at inference, or overfitting the validation set."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#6","src":"c","q":"How do you pick k in K-Means?","a":"<p>Elbow (inertia), silhouette score, gap statistic, domain needs.</p>\n","s":"Elbow (inertia), silhouette score, gap statistic, and what the business can use."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#7","src":"c","q":"Is accuracy always good?","a":"<p>No; with imbalance use precision/recall/PR-AUC/F1.</p>\n","s":"No: with imbalance use precision, recall, PR-AUC, F1 and cost-based metrics."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#8","src":"c","q":"How do trees handle missing values?","a":"<p>Depends: XGBoost/LightGBM learn default direction; sklearn (recent versions) supports NaN in some tree models; otherwise impute.</p>\n","s":"XGBoost/LightGBM learn a default direction; newer scikit-learn supports NaN in some trees; otherwise impute."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#9","src":"c","q":"Explain precision-recall tradeoff.","a":"<p>Raising threshold increases precision, decreases recall, and vice versa.</p>\n","s":"Raising the threshold raises precision and lowers recall, and vice versa."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#10","src":"c","q":"How to explain a black-box model?","a":"<p>Feature importance, permutation importance, PDP/ICE, SHAP, LIME, surrogate models.</p>\n","s":"Feature and permutation importance, PDP/ICE, SHAP, LIME, or a surrogate model."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#11","src":"c","q":"Bagging vs boosting vs stacking?","a":"<p>See <a href=\"#/s/7.3.5\">7.3.5</a>/7.3.9.</p>\n","s":"Bagging averages parallel models (variance), boosting fits sequentially (bias), stacking learns a meta-model over model outputs."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#12","src":"c","q":"Difference between parameters and hyperparameters?","a":"<p>Parameters learned from data (weights, split points); hyperparameters set before training (depth, lr).</p>\n","s":"Parameters are learned from the data; hyperparameters are set before training."},{"k":"qa","id":"7.6#13","src":"c","q":"What is the difference between <code>fit</code>, <code>transform</code>, <code>fit_transform</code>?","a":"<p><code>fit</code> learns statistics from data; <code>transform</code> applies them; <code>fit_transform</code> does both (only on train).</p>\n","s":"<code>fit</code> learns statistics from data, <code>transform</code> applies them, <code>fit_transform</code> does both (only on training data)."},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-7-7\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.7</span> Self-check (Module 7)</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m7-qa.c1","kind":"self","items":["Explain each algorithm in 2 minutes: intuition, loss, key hyperparameters, pros/cons.","Implement logistic regression and k-means from scratch.","For a given business problem, justify the algorithm choice."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-extra-questions-with-model-answers\">Extra questions with model answers</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-qa~1","q":"How do you handle missing values in tree-based vs linear models?","a":"<p>Gradient-boosting libraries such as XGBoost and LightGBM learn a <strong>default branch</strong> for missing values, and recent scikit-learn tree models also accept NaN; random forests in older setups need imputation. Linear and distance-based models need explicit <strong>imputation</strong> (median or group-wise), usually with a <strong>missing indicator</strong> feature, because the fact that a value is missing may itself be informative. Always fit imputers on training data only, and monitor the missing rate in production.</p>\n","s":"Trees (XGBoost, LightGBM) can learn a default direction; linear models need imputation plus a missing-indicator."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-qa~2","q":"What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter, with examples?","a":"<p><strong>Parameters</strong> are learned by the training algorithm (regression weights, tree split thresholds, neural-network weights). <strong>Hyperparameters</strong> control the learning process or model capacity and are chosen by you or by search (tree depth, learning rate, regularisation strength, <code>k</code> in KNN). Hyperparameters are tuned on validation data, parameters on training data.</p>\n","s":"Parameters are learned from data; hyperparameters are set before training."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m7-qa~3","q":"Your classification model has AUC 0.85. Can you tell whether it is good enough?","a":"<p>AUC summarises ranking quality independent of threshold, but &quot;good enough&quot; depends on the baseline (logistic regression, current rules), class balance (PR-AUC may be more informative), the <strong>cost of each error</strong>, calibration, and performance in important segments. Report precision/recall (or lift) at the <strong>operating threshold</strong> and the expected business value, and check stability over time.</p>\n","s":"Not without a baseline and the business cost: compare with a simple model, check calibration, and evaluate at the operating threshold."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-self-check-from-the-course\">Self-check (from the course)</h3>\n<p>Use the checklist above. A strong pass means you can, without notes: explain each algorithm in two minutes (intuition, loss, key hyperparameters, pros and cons), implement logistic regression and k-means from scratch, and justify an algorithm choice for a business problem.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-qa~ca0_1","q":"Give the four-part answer for &quot;why does regularisation reduce overfitting?&quot;","a":"<p>Concept: it penalises large weights. Mechanism: it limits model flexibility so it cannot fit noise. Example: ridge shrinks coefficients on noisy features. Trade-off: too much causes underfitting, tuned by validation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-qa~ca0_2","q":"An interviewer asks &quot;which model would you use?&quot; with no details. What do you do?","a":"<p>Ask about the data size, features, target, interpretability and latency needs; then propose a baseline and a stronger option with reasons.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m7-qa~ca0_3","q":"How do you diagnose a model with strong offline metrics but poor production results?","a":"<p>Check leakage, train-serve skew, distribution drift, label delay and whether the offline metric matches the business objective.</p>\n"}]}});